var e={level:`B2`,type:`true-false`,questions:[{id:`b2-tf-001`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'save time and money', 'managers often worry' and 'feeling isolated'.`,passage:`Remote working has changed the way many companies operate. Employees say they save time and money because they do not need to travel. However, managers often worry that staff are less productive at home, and some workers report feeling isolated from their colleagues.`,statements:[{text:`Employees say remote working saves them time.`,answer:!0},{text:`All managers believe staff work better at home.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some workers feel isolated.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-002`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'almost empty twenty years ago' and 'a group of young artists moved in'.`,passage:`The village of Alden was almost empty twenty years ago. Then a group of young artists moved in and opened small galleries. Today the village attracts thousands of visitors every summer, although local residents complain about the noise and the high prices.`,statements:[{text:`Alden is less popular than it was twenty years ago.`,answer:!1},{text:`Artists played a part in the change.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-003`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'some older customers were unhappy', 'busier than ever', 'remains the best-selling item' and 'has not found the right building yet'.`,passage:`When Maria Lopez took over her grandfather's bakery in a small town near the coast, most people expected her to keep things exactly as they were. Instead, she introduced gluten-free bread and started selling coffee in the mornings. At first, some older customers were unhappy and stopped coming. However, within a year the shop had become busier than ever, mainly because young families and tourists began to visit. Maria still uses her grandfather's recipe for the traditional loaf, which remains the best-selling item. She admits that if she had listened to everyone's advice, she would never have made any changes. Next spring she is planning to open a second shop in the nearby city, although she has not found the right building yet.`,statements:[{text:`Maria made changes that some regular customers did not like.`,answer:!0},{text:`The bakery lost money during Maria's first year.`,answer:!1},{text:`The traditional loaf is no longer on sale.`,answer:!1},{text:`Maria has already chosen a building for her second shop.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-004`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'lives under the sea ice', 'depend on the cod for most of their diet', 'three winters' and 'limit fishing'.`,passage:`Scientists working in the Arctic have discovered that a small fish called the polar cod is far more important to the local food chain than previously thought. The fish lives under the sea ice and feeds on tiny animals that grow there. Seals, whales and seabirds, in turn, depend on the cod for most of their diet. As the ice melts earlier each year, the cod are losing their main hiding place and source of food. Researchers warn that if the population falls sharply, many larger animals could struggle to survive. The team spent three winters collecting data, often working in temperatures below minus thirty degrees. They hope their findings will persuade governments to limit fishing in the area until more is known.`,statements:[{text:`The polar cod lives beneath the sea ice.`,answer:!0},{text:`Seals and whales rely heavily on the polar cod for food.`,answer:!0},{text:`The researchers completed their work in a single winter.`,answer:!1},{text:`The scientists want fishing in the area to be increased.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-005`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'the real figure depends on the person', 'someone who exercises a lot... will need more', 'tea and coffee count too' and 'drink when you feel thirsty'.`,passage:`Many people believe that you need eight glasses of water a day to stay healthy, but doctors say the real figure depends on the person. Someone who exercises a lot or lives in a hot country will obviously need more than an office worker in a cool climate. It is also worth remembering that food provides a surprising amount of water. Fruit and vegetables such as cucumbers, oranges and tomatoes are mostly water, and even bread contains some. Tea and coffee count too, despite the popular idea that they dry you out. The best advice, according to most experts, is simply to drink when you feel thirsty and to check the colour of your urine: if it is pale, you are probably drinking enough.`,statements:[{text:`Doctors agree that everyone needs exactly eight glasses of water a day.`,answer:!1},{text:`People who do a lot of sport usually need more water.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to the text, tea and coffee do not count towards your daily water.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts suggest drinking when you are thirsty.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-006`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'first tested last summer', 'most of them reported higher sales', 'residents... complain' and 'free parking spaces'.`,passage:`The city of Valmont has decided to ban cars from its historic centre on Sundays. The idea was first tested last summer, when the main square and the streets around it were closed to traffic for four weekends. Shop owners had been worried that they would lose customers, but most of them reported higher sales than usual, as people stayed longer and walked from shop to shop. Cyclists and families with children were especially enthusiastic. Not everyone is happy, though. Residents who live in the centre complain that they will have to park their cars outside the area and carry their shopping home. The council has promised to provide free parking spaces for them and a small electric bus that will run every fifteen minutes.`,statements:[{text:`The car ban was tested before the final decision was made.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most shop owners sold less during the test period.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some people living in the centre are unhappy with the decision.`,answer:!0},{text:`The council will charge residents for parking outside the centre.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-007`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'he almost refused', his talk was about 'mountain biking', members told him 'to slow down' and 'he wishes he had joined the club years earlier'.`,passage:`Daniel had always been afraid of public speaking, so when his manager asked him to present the team's results at the annual conference, he almost refused. Instead, he decided to join a local speaking club that met every Tuesday evening. For the first few weeks, he only listened to other members, but eventually he gave a short talk about his favourite hobby, mountain biking. The other members gave him helpful feedback, telling him to slow down and look at the audience. By the time the conference came, Daniel had practised his presentation at least ten times. He was still nervous on the day, but the talk went well and several people asked him questions afterwards. He now says he wishes he had joined the club years earlier.`,statements:[{text:`Daniel accepted his manager's request without hesitation.`,answer:!1},{text:`Daniel's first talk at the club was about his work.`,answer:!1},{text:`Club members advised Daniel to speak more slowly.`,answer:!0},{text:`Daniel regrets not joining the club sooner.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-008`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says officials 'tried to reduce the number of stalls', critics say it 'would destroy part of the city's character', and vendors 'must clean the area before they leave'.`,passage:`Street food has become one of the main attractions for visitors to Bangkok, but in recent years the city authorities have tried to reduce the number of stalls on busy pavements. Officials argue that the stalls block the way for pedestrians and create problems with rubbish. Food lovers, on the other hand, say that removing the stalls would destroy part of the city's character. In some areas, a compromise has been reached: vendors are allowed to sell food only at certain times of the day, usually in the evening, and they must clean the area before they leave. Many tourists now plan their trips around these night markets, where they can try dozens of dishes at low prices. For many vendors, these sales are their family's only income.`,statements:[{text:`The authorities want to increase the number of food stalls.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some people think removing the stalls would harm the city's identity.`,answer:!0},{text:`In some areas vendors can only sell food at certain times.`,answer:!0},{text:`Vendors are not responsible for cleaning after they finish.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-009`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the first group worked 'apart from a lunch break', breaks were 'not to check their phones', the second group 'made fewer mistakes' and 'the study was small'.`,passage:`A new study suggests that taking short breaks during the working day can make people more productive. Researchers followed two groups of office workers for six weeks. The first group worked without stopping, apart from a lunch break, while the second group was asked to take a five-minute break every hour. During these breaks, they were encouraged to stand up, stretch or look out of the window, but not to check their phones. At the end of the study, the second group had completed more tasks and made fewer mistakes. They also said they felt less tired at the end of the day. The researchers admit that the study was small, and they are now planning a larger one involving several companies in different countries.`,statements:[{text:`Both groups of workers were allowed a lunch break.`,answer:!0},{text:`The second group was encouraged to use their phones during breaks.`,answer:!1},{text:`Workers who took regular breaks made more mistakes.`,answer:!1},{text:`The researchers believe their study was too small to be final.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-010`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she worked 'part-time at the hospital' while studying, friends 'thought she was taking a big risk', she 'earned much less' at first, and nursing 'taught her how to listen'.`,passage:`Olivia Grant was a nurse for twenty years before she decided to change careers at the age of forty-five. She had always loved gardening, so she enrolled on a two-year course in landscape design while still working part-time at the hospital. Many of her friends thought she was taking a big risk, especially as she had two teenage children. After finishing the course, Olivia started her own business designing small city gardens. The first year was difficult, and she earned much less than she had as a nurse. Now, five years later, she employs three people and has more work than she can manage. She says that her experience in nursing taught her how to listen to people, which is essential when designing a garden for someone.`,statements:[{text:`Olivia stopped working at the hospital completely while she studied.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some of Olivia's friends were worried about her decision.`,answer:!0},{text:`Olivia earned more in her first year of business than she had as a nurse.`,answer:!1},{text:`Olivia thinks her nursing experience helps her in her new job.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-011`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says these women 'never go to matches', alcohol is banned only in 'family sections', a Dutch club offers 'free tickets to girls under twelve', and attendance 'has risen by around fifteen per cent'.`,passage:`Football clubs in several European countries are trying new ways to attract female fans to their stadiums. Research carried out last year showed that many women who enjoy watching football on television never go to matches because they feel the atmosphere is unwelcoming. In response, some clubs have introduced family sections where alcohol is not allowed, and others have improved facilities such as toilets and lighting around the stadium. One club in the Netherlands has started offering free tickets to girls under twelve who play in local teams. Early results are encouraging: the number of women attending matches has risen by around fifteen per cent at the clubs involved. Club officials say they would like to see the same changes across the whole league within five years.`,statements:[{text:`Many women who like football on TV do not go to matches.`,answer:!0},{text:`All clubs have banned alcohol across the whole stadium.`,answer:!1},{text:`A Dutch club gives free tickets to some young players.`,answer:!0},{text:`The number of female fans has fallen at the clubs involved.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-012`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'more than half said they used their phones in bed', the survey 'cannot prove that phones cause poor sleep', schools advise keeping phones 'out of bedrooms', and children 'seem more relaxed'.`,passage:`Smartphones are often blamed for keeping teenagers awake at night, and a recent survey seems to support this idea. Out of two thousand students aged between thirteen and eighteen, more than half said they used their phones in bed, and almost a third admitted waking up during the night to check messages. Those who did so reported feeling tired at school and finding it harder to concentrate. However, the researchers point out that the survey cannot prove that phones cause poor sleep; it is possible that teenagers who already sleep badly simply use their phones more. Some schools have started advising parents to keep phones out of bedrooms after ten o'clock. A few parents say this rule has led to arguments at first, but most report that their children seem more relaxed.`,statements:[{text:`More than half of the students used their phones in bed.`,answer:!0},{text:`The survey proves that phones cause poor sleep.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some schools suggest removing phones from bedrooms at night.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to the text, most parents found the rule made their children more stressed.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-013`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it 'started as a gathering... in a pub', it 'has grown into' a large event, it 'has kept its focus on traditional folk music', and the campsite is 'for the first time' this year.`,passage:`Every August, the small Scottish town of Kirkmuir hosts a music festival that started as a gathering of a few local musicians in a pub. Over thirty years, it has grown into an event that attracts around twenty thousand people. Unlike many large festivals, Kirkmuir has kept its focus on traditional folk music, although organisers have recently added a stage for younger bands who mix folk with rock and electronic sounds. Most of the work is done by volunteers, many of whom live in the town. The festival brings a lot of money into the area, but it also causes problems, such as traffic jams and a shortage of accommodation. This year, for the first time, the organisers have set up a campsite on a farm outside the town.`,statements:[{text:`The festival began in a pub.`,answer:!0},{text:`The festival has always attracted around twenty thousand people.`,answer:!1},{text:`Organisers have stopped including traditional folk music.`,answer:!1},{text:`A campsite has been provided before this year.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-014`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says users 'leave it there' at their destination, injuries were 'mostly among riders who were not wearing helmets', Paris residents 'decided to ban rented scooters', and supporters say 'better rules, not bans'.`,passage:`Electric scooters have appeared on the streets of many cities, and they are popular with commuters who want a quick, cheap way to travel short distances. Users can unlock a scooter with an app, ride it to their destination and leave it there. However, the scooters have also caused a number of problems. Pedestrians complain about scooters being left in the middle of pavements, and hospitals have reported an increase in injuries, mostly among riders who were not wearing helmets. Some cities have responded by limiting the maximum speed to twenty kilometres per hour and creating special parking areas. Paris went further and held a public vote in which residents decided to ban rented scooters altogether. Supporters of the scooters argue that better rules, not bans, are the answer.`,statements:[{text:`Scooter users must return the scooter to the place where they rented it.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most injuries involved riders without helmets.`,answer:!0},{text:`Paris residents voted to ban rented scooters.`,answer:!0},{text:`Supporters of scooters believe bans are the best solution.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-015`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the journey 'took almost seven hours', 'many experts said it could not be done', the line opened 'just in time for the Olympic Games', and lines have been built 'in many countries'.`,passage:`In the 1960s, a group of engineers at a small company in Japan was asked to design a train that could travel between Tokyo and Osaka in under four hours. At the time, the journey took almost seven hours. Many experts said it could not be done, as no train in the world had ever reached the speeds that would be needed. Nevertheless, the engineers succeeded, and the first high-speed line opened in 1964, just in time for the Olympic Games held in Tokyo that year. The new trains were not only fast but also extremely punctual, and they soon became a symbol of modern Japan. Since then, high-speed railways have been built in many countries, including France, Spain and China.`,statements:[{text:`Before the new line, the journey between Tokyo and Osaka took about seven hours.`,answer:!0},{text:`All experts were confident that the project would succeed.`,answer:!1},{text:`The first high-speed line opened before the Tokyo Olympic Games.`,answer:!0},{text:`Japan is the only country with high-speed railways.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-016`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'she felt lonely', 'her landlady suggested' the group, members were 'people of all ages', and 'she still volunteers twice a month'.`,passage:`Lena moved to Berlin from a small village in Austria to study architecture. During her first month, she felt lonely because she did not know anyone and found the city overwhelming. Her landlady suggested she should join a volunteer group that cleaned up parks at weekends. Lena was not very enthusiastic, but she agreed to try it once. To her surprise, she enjoyed it a lot. The group included people of all ages and from many different countries, and after each clean-up they usually had lunch together. Through the group, Lena met her two closest friends and even found a part-time job in a café run by one of the members. She still volunteers twice a month, even though her studies keep her very busy.`,statements:[{text:`Lena felt lonely when she first arrived in Berlin.`,answer:!0},{text:`Lena's landlady suggested the volunteer group.`,answer:!0},{text:`Everyone in the volunteer group was a student.`,answer:!1},{text:`Lena has stopped volunteering because she is too busy.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-017`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says coffee grows 'near the equator', land 'could be reduced by half', farmers move 'higher up the mountains', and some wild species 'do not taste as good'.`,passage:`Coffee is one of the most traded products in the world, but climate change could make it much harder to grow. Most coffee is produced in a narrow band of countries near the equator, where temperatures and rainfall are just right for the plants. As temperatures rise, many of these areas could become too hot or too dry. Some studies suggest that by 2050 the land suitable for growing coffee could be reduced by half. Farmers are already trying to adapt by moving their plantations higher up the mountains, where it is cooler, or by planting trees to provide shade. Scientists are also testing wild coffee species that can survive higher temperatures, although some of them do not taste as good as the varieties people drink today.`,statements:[{text:`Most coffee is grown in countries close to the equator.`,answer:!0},{text:`Studies show that coffee production will certainly double by 2050.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some farmers are moving their plantations to higher ground.`,answer:!0},{text:`All the wild coffee species being tested taste better than current varieties.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-018`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'without losing any pay', 'the number of sick days fell', restaurants 'find it more difficult to organise', and 'more than ninety per cent decided to continue'.`,passage:`Many companies now offer their employees the chance to work a four-day week without losing any pay. Supporters say that staff become more focused and efficient when they know they have an extra day off. In a trial involving sixty British companies, most firms reported that productivity stayed the same or even improved, and the number of sick days fell. Workers said they had more time for their families and hobbies. However, the system does not suit every business. Hospitals, shops and restaurants, which need staff every day, find it more difficult to organise. Some managers also worry that customers might be unhappy if they cannot contact the company on Fridays. Of the companies in the trial, more than ninety per cent decided to continue with the four-day week.`,statements:[{text:`Employees in the trial worked fewer days for less money.`,answer:!1},{text:`The number of days workers were ill went down.`,answer:!0},{text:`The text suggests the four-day week is easy for restaurants to organise.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most companies in the trial kept the new system.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-019`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says queues form 'when the weather is good', waiting 'can be very dangerous', waste does 'not decompose at such low temperatures', and climbers 'lose their deposit'.`,passage:`Mount Everest has become so popular with climbers that it now suffers from serious overcrowding. On days when the weather is good, hundreds of people try to reach the summit, and long queues form on the narrow paths near the top. Waiting in such extreme cold and with so little oxygen can be very dangerous. In 2019, several climbers died after spending hours in a queue. Another problem is waste: climbers leave behind tents, oxygen bottles and other rubbish, which do not decompose at such low temperatures. The government of Nepal now requires every climber to bring down at least eight kilograms of rubbish, or they lose their deposit. Some people have suggested limiting the number of permits, but this would reduce an important source of income for the country.`,statements:[{text:`Queues near the summit only form when the weather is bad.`,answer:!1},{text:`Waiting near the top of Everest can be life-threatening.`,answer:!0},{text:`Rubbish on Everest breaks down quickly because of the cold.`,answer:!1},{text:`Climbers who do not bring down rubbish can lose money.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-020`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says he opened it 'after losing his job', he sells 'at fair prices', the workshops are 'free', and he 'has had to introduce a booking system'.`,passage:`Ahmed opened a small bicycle repair shop in his neighbourhood five years ago, after losing his job at a car factory. At first he only fixed bikes, but he soon noticed that many people had old bicycles at home that they never used. He started buying these bicycles cheaply, repairing them and selling them at fair prices to students and people on low incomes. He also runs free workshops on Saturday mornings, where he teaches people how to fix punctures and adjust their brakes. The workshops have become so popular that he has had to introduce a booking system. Last year the local council gave Ahmed an award for his contribution to the community, and a national newspaper wrote an article about him.`,statements:[{text:`Ahmed opened his shop after he lost his previous job.`,answer:!0},{text:`Ahmed sells repaired bicycles at very high prices.`,answer:!1},{text:`People have to pay to attend Ahmed's Saturday workshops.`,answer:!1},{text:`Ahmed's workshops are now so popular that people need to book.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-021`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says headsets show 'what an ancient city looked like', the game is for 'children', visits from families 'have increased', and museums keep 'technology optional'.`,passage:`Museums around the world are using new technology to attract younger visitors. Some offer virtual reality headsets that allow people to see what an ancient city looked like thousands of years ago, while others have created apps that tell stories about the objects on display. The Natural History Museum in one European capital recently introduced a game in which children search the building for hidden clues to solve a mystery. Staff say that visits from families have increased considerably since the game was launched. However, not everyone welcomes these changes. Some critics argue that screens distract visitors from the real objects and that museums should encourage people to look closely rather than through a device. Most museums try to find a balance, keeping technology optional.`,statements:[{text:`Some museums let visitors see ancient cities using virtual reality.`,answer:!0},{text:`The game introduced by the Natural History Museum is designed for adults.`,answer:!1},{text:`Family visits went down after the game was launched.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most museums make technology optional for visitors.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-022`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says fermentation is 'one of the oldest ways', 'more research is needed', heating 'kills them', and the process 'can take several weeks'.`,passage:`Fermented foods such as yoghurt, kimchi and sauerkraut have become fashionable in recent years, partly because of claims that they are good for the stomach. Fermentation is one of the oldest ways of preserving food: bacteria or yeast change the sugars in the food, which helps it last longer and gives it a sour taste. Some scientists believe the bacteria in these foods can improve digestion and even strengthen the immune system, but they stress that more research is needed. Not all products sold in supermarkets contain live bacteria, since some are heated during production, which kills them. Experts therefore recommend checking the label carefully. Making fermented vegetables at home is cheap and fairly easy, although it requires patience, as the process can take several weeks.`,statements:[{text:`Fermentation is a modern method of preserving food.`,answer:!1},{text:`Scientists say there is already enough evidence about the health benefits.`,answer:!1},{text:`Heating some products during production kills the bacteria.`,answer:!0},{text:`Making fermented vegetables at home can take weeks.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-023`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the building 'stood empty for almost twenty years', plans to knock it down 'were rejected', the brick walls and columns 'were kept', and 'a quarter of the flats' have lower rents.`,passage:`When the old textile factory in the city of Lowfield closed in the 1990s, the building stood empty for almost twenty years. Several plans to knock it down were rejected because local people wanted to keep it as part of the town's history. Finally, a group of investors bought it and turned it into a centre with flats, offices, a cinema and a covered market. The original brick walls and iron columns were kept, and a small museum on the ground floor tells the story of the factory and its workers. The project was criticised at first because the flats were too expensive for most local families. Following these complaints, the developers agreed that a quarter of the flats would be offered at lower rents.`,statements:[{text:`The factory building was used again soon after it closed.`,answer:!1},{text:`Local people opposed plans to demolish the building.`,answer:!0},{text:`None of the original features of the building were kept.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some of the flats are now available at reduced rents.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-024`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it is 'far from impossible', adults 'may feel embarrassed about making mistakes', teachers prefer 'short periods every day', and playing can 'reduce stress'.`,passage:`Learning a musical instrument as an adult is harder than learning as a child, but research shows it is far from impossible. Adults usually have less free time and may feel embarrassed about making mistakes, which can make them give up quickly. On the other hand, they often have better concentration and a clearer idea of why they want to learn. Teachers recommend practising for short periods every day rather than for several hours once a week. They also suggest choosing music you genuinely enjoy, since motivation is the key to making progress. Studies have found that playing an instrument can improve memory and reduce stress at any age. Many adult learners say that joining a group, such as a community orchestra, helped them keep going.`,statements:[{text:`Research suggests adults cannot learn a musical instrument.`,answer:!1},{text:`Adults may give up because they are embarrassed about their mistakes.`,answer:!0},{text:`Teachers recommend one long practice session per week.`,answer:!1},{text:`Playing an instrument can reduce stress.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-025`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says residents were 'most of them elderly', 'the internet connection was extremely slow', the school 'now has fifteen pupils', and newcomers 'helped to reopen the island's only shop'.`,passage:`The island of Hallsey, off the west coast of Ireland, had only about fifty permanent residents ten years ago, most of them elderly. Young people kept leaving because there were no jobs and the internet connection was extremely slow. Then the island received a grant to install high-speed broadband. Since then, around twenty families have moved there, many of them people who work online for companies based in cities. The primary school, which was about to close, now has fifteen pupils. Not all residents are pleased: some worry that house prices are rising and that the island's quiet way of life could change. The newcomers, however, have helped to reopen the island's only shop and organise events such as a summer food fair.`,statements:[{text:`Ten years ago most of the island's residents were young.`,answer:!1},{text:`Poor internet was one reason young people left the island.`,answer:!0},{text:`The primary school closed after the broadband was installed.`,answer:!1},{text:`New residents helped to reopen the shop.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-026`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says intense exercise 'can make it harder to fall asleep', keep the same times 'even at weekends', sleeping late on Sunday makes Monday 'more difficult', and see a doctor if problems 'continue for more than a few weeks'.`,passage:`Sleep experts say that the hour before bed is just as important as the sleep itself. Bright screens, heavy meals and intense exercise can all make it harder to fall asleep. Instead, they recommend activities that help the body relax, such as reading a paper book, having a warm shower or listening to calm music. Keeping a regular schedule is also important: going to bed and getting up at the same time every day, even at weekends, helps the body's internal clock work properly. Many people try to catch up on sleep at the weekend, but experts warn that sleeping very late on Sunday can make Monday morning even more difficult. If sleep problems continue for more than a few weeks, it is a good idea to see a doctor.`,statements:[{text:`Intense exercise just before bed can make it harder to sleep.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts advise changing your sleep schedule at weekends.`,answer:!1},{text:`Sleeping very late on Sunday can make Monday morning harder.`,answer:!0},{text:`People with sleep problems should see a doctor immediately after one bad night.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-027`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says robots deliver items 'to human workers, who then pack them', orders are processed 'three times faster', unions say 'many workers lost their jobs', and humans 'will focus on work that needs creativity'.`,passage:`Robots are increasingly being used in warehouses to pick and pack goods for online orders. In one large warehouse in the north of England, robots move along a grid on top of storage boxes, lifting the items needed and delivering them to human workers, who then pack them. The company says this system allows it to process orders three times faster than before. Although some jobs have disappeared, the company claims that it has retrained many employees to maintain and repair the robots. Trade unions, however, say that many workers lost their jobs and that the new roles require skills not everyone can easily learn. Experts believe that in the future, robots will take over more routine tasks, while humans will focus on work that needs creativity and judgement.`,statements:[{text:`In the warehouse described, robots do all the packing without human help.`,answer:!1},{text:`The company says orders are processed much faster with robots.`,answer:!0},{text:`Trade unions agree that no workers lost their jobs.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts think humans will focus on creative work in the future.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-028`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says bees 'pollinate many of the crops we eat', 'there is no single cause', people 'keep beehives on rooftops', and wild bees are 'just as important'.`,passage:`Honeybees play a vital role in agriculture because they pollinate many of the crops we eat, including apples, almonds and strawberries. In recent decades, however, beekeepers in Europe and North America have reported losing large numbers of colonies every winter. Scientists believe there is no single cause. Pesticides, diseases, a lack of wild flowers and changes in the weather all seem to play a part. To help, some cities have started planting flowers along roads and in parks, and many people now keep beehives on rooftops. Experts point out, though, that wild bees, which are often forgotten, are just as important as honeybees and may be even more at risk. They encourage gardeners to leave some areas wild and avoid using chemicals.`,statements:[{text:`Honeybees help many food crops to grow.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientists have identified one main cause of colony losses.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some people keep beehives on the roofs of buildings.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts say wild bees are less important than honeybees.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-029`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she bought it 'on a sudden impulse', learned skills 'from her neighbours', 'the roof leaked' in the first winter, and she has 'no plans to return to London'.`,passage:`Ten years ago, Sophie Chen was working long hours as an accountant in London when she went on holiday to Portugal and fell in love with a small village in the hills. On a sudden impulse, she bought an old farmhouse that needed a lot of repairs. She spent the next three years restoring it, mostly by herself, learning skills such as plastering and tiling from online videos and from her neighbours. The farmhouse is now a guesthouse with six rooms, and it is fully booked for most of the summer. Sophie admits that there were moments when she regretted the decision, especially during the first winter, when the roof leaked. But she says she would do it all again, and she has no plans to return to London.`,statements:[{text:`Sophie bought the farmhouse after planning carefully for many years.`,answer:!1},{text:`Sophie learned some building skills from her neighbours.`,answer:!0},{text:`The roof of the farmhouse leaked during the first winter.`,answer:!0},{text:`Sophie is planning to move back to London.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-030`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the food is 'still safe to eat', it is sold 'at a discount', users collect bags 'often without knowing exactly what will be inside', and critics say 'the real problem is overproduction'.`,passage:`Many supermarkets throw away large amounts of food that is still safe to eat, simply because it has passed its 'best before' date or does not look perfect. In response, a number of apps have been developed that connect shops and restaurants with customers who want to buy surplus food at a discount. At the end of the day, a bakery might offer a bag of unsold bread and pastries for a third of the normal price. Users collect their bag at a set time, often without knowing exactly what will be inside. The companies behind these apps say they have saved millions of meals from being wasted. Critics, however, argue that the real problem is overproduction and that shops should simply produce less.`,statements:[{text:`Some food that supermarkets throw away is still safe to eat.`,answer:!0},{text:`The apps allow customers to buy surplus food at reduced prices.`,answer:!0},{text:`Users always know exactly what they will receive.`,answer:!1},{text:`Critics believe overproduction is the real problem.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-031`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says races 'receive far more applications than they have places', most runners 'are not trying to win', increasing distance too quickly 'often leads to injury', and experts 'stress the importance of rest days'.`,passage:`Marathon running has become hugely popular among amateurs, and big-city races now receive far more applications than they have places. In some cases, runners have to enter a ballot, and only one in ten applicants is selected. Most participants are not trying to win; they want to raise money for charity or simply prove to themselves that they can finish. Doctors say that training for a marathon can bring great health benefits, but they warn beginners not to increase their distance too quickly, as this often leads to injury. A typical training plan lasts around four months and includes one long run each week. Experts also stress the importance of rest days, which allow the muscles to recover and become stronger.`,statements:[{text:`Big-city marathons have more places than applicants.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most amateur runners are trying to win the race.`,answer:!1},{text:`Increasing distance too fast can cause injuries.`,answer:!0},{text:`Rest days are considered unimportant by experts.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-032`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people used to stay 'with one company for most of their working lives', young workers value flexibility 'more than a high salary', some allow staff to 'choose their own hours', and 'some employers are suspicious'.`,passage:`In the past, most people stayed with one company for most of their working lives. Today, however, it is common for workers to change jobs every few years. Surveys suggest that younger employees in particular value flexibility, learning opportunities and a good work-life balance more than a high salary. Companies have noticed this and are trying harder to keep their staff. Some offer training programmes, while others allow employees to work from home or choose their own hours. Changing jobs frequently can help people gain a wide range of experience and often leads to higher pay. On the other hand, some employers are suspicious of candidates whose CVs show many short jobs, as they fear these people will not stay long.`,statements:[{text:`In the past, people usually changed jobs every few years.`,answer:!1},{text:`Younger workers often care more about flexibility than salary.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some companies let employees choose their own working hours.`,answer:!0},{text:`All employers see frequent job changes as a positive sign.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-033`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says tomatoes came 'from Central and South America', 'many Europeans refused to eat it', the fear in Britain 'lasted longer', and botanists 'classify it as a fruit'.`,passage:`The tomato was brought to Europe from Central and South America in the sixteenth century. At first, many Europeans refused to eat it. Because the plant belongs to the same family as some poisonous plants, people grew it only for decoration. In Italy and Spain, however, cooks gradually began to use it in sauces, and by the eighteenth century it had become a common ingredient in southern European cooking. In Britain and North America, the fear lasted longer, and it was not until the nineteenth century that tomatoes were widely eaten there. Today, the tomato is one of the most widely grown vegetables in the world, although botanists actually classify it as a fruit because it contains seeds.`,statements:[{text:`Tomatoes originally came from the Americas.`,answer:!0},{text:`Europeans began eating tomatoes as soon as they arrived.`,answer:!1},{text:`Tomatoes were accepted in southern Europe before they were in Britain.`,answer:!0},{text:`Botanists consider the tomato to be a vegetable.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-034`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says users can 'compete with friends', research 'is mixed' and one group 'lost slightly less weight', doctors worry it 'can make people anxious', and experts say trackers 'can be useful tools'.`,passage:`Wearable fitness trackers, which count steps, measure heart rate and monitor sleep, are now worn by millions of people. Many users say the devices motivate them to move more, especially when they can compete with friends. However, research on their long-term effects is mixed. One study found that people who wore a tracker for two years lost slightly less weight than those who did not, possibly because they felt they had earned the right to eat more after reaching their step target. Some doctors also worry that constant monitoring can make people anxious about their health. Most experts agree that trackers can be useful tools, but only if people use the information sensibly rather than becoming obsessed with numbers.`,statements:[{text:`Some users are motivated by competing with friends.`,answer:!0},{text:`Research clearly shows that trackers help people lose more weight.`,answer:!1},{text:`Doctors are concerned that monitoring may cause anxiety.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts believe trackers are useless.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-035`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people cycle 'whatever the weather', cycling 'was becoming less common', lanes are 'separated from traffic', and other cities 'have sent planners to Copenhagen'.`,passage:`Copenhagen is often described as one of the most bicycle-friendly cities in the world. More than forty per cent of residents cycle to work or school every day, whatever the weather. This was not always the case. In the 1960s and 1970s, the city was full of cars, and cycling was becoming less common. After the oil crisis of the 1970s, however, residents began demanding safer routes for bikes. The city responded by building wide cycle lanes separated from traffic, special bridges for cyclists and traffic lights timed to suit bikes. Today, local politicians say that cycling saves the city millions in health care costs each year. Other cities, including some in North America, have sent planners to Copenhagen to learn from its experience.`,statements:[{text:`Copenhagen residents only cycle when the weather is good.`,answer:!1},{text:`Cycling became less common in Copenhagen in the 1960s and 1970s.`,answer:!0},{text:`The city built cycle lanes that are separated from cars.`,answer:!0},{text:`Planners from other countries have visited Copenhagen to learn from it.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-036`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says he had painted 'at school', his teacher 'encouraged him to enter', his painting 'won second prize', and he gave 'some of the money to a charity'.`,passage:`When Thomas retired from his job as a postman, he was worried about being bored. His daughter suggested he should take up painting, something he had enjoyed at school but had not done for over forty years. He joined an evening class at the local college and was surprised to find that he had not lost his skill. After a year, his teacher encouraged him to enter a regional competition for amateur artists. Thomas did not expect to win anything, but his painting of the harbour at sunrise won second prize. Since then, he has sold several paintings at a local gallery and given some of the money to a charity for older people. He now says that retirement has been the busiest time of his life.`,statements:[{text:`Thomas had never painted before he retired.`,answer:!1},{text:`His teacher suggested he should enter a competition.`,answer:!0},{text:`Thomas won first prize in the competition.`,answer:!1},{text:`Thomas has given some of his earnings to charity.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-037`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the soil 'is extremely fertile', Iceland uses underground heat 'to produce electricity', scientists 'cannot predict eruptions exactly', and their warnings 'have saved many lives'.`,passage:`Volcanoes are often seen only as dangerous, but they also bring benefits. The soil around many volcanoes is extremely fertile, which is why farmers have lived on their slopes for thousands of years, despite the risks. In countries such as Iceland, the heat from underground is used to produce electricity and to warm homes. Volcanic areas also attract large numbers of tourists, who come to see craters, hot springs and lava fields. Scientists monitor active volcanoes closely, measuring small earthquakes and changes in the shape of the ground, which can show that an eruption may be coming. Although they cannot predict eruptions exactly, their warnings have saved many lives by allowing authorities to evacuate people in time.`,statements:[{text:`The soil near volcanoes is usually poor for farming.`,answer:!1},{text:`Iceland uses underground heat to produce electricity.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientists can predict the exact time of an eruption.`,answer:!1},{text:`Scientists' warnings have helped to save lives.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-038`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says pupils grow vegetables 'in the school garden', fussy eaters 'are now willing to try new foods', some children 'ask to help cook at home', and 'the main obstacle... is cost'.`,passage:`A growing number of schools are teaching children to cook as part of their regular timetable. In one primary school in Manchester, pupils spend an hour every week in a kitchen classroom, where they learn to prepare simple meals using fresh vegetables, many of which they grow themselves in the school garden. Teachers say that children who were once fussy eaters are now willing to try new foods because they helped to make them. Parents have also noticed changes, with some reporting that their children now ask to help cook at home. The head teacher believes that cooking also teaches maths, science and teamwork. The main obstacle for other schools is cost, as kitchen equipment is expensive and lessons require extra staff.`,statements:[{text:`Pupils grow some of the vegetables they use.`,answer:!0},{text:`Teachers say fussy eaters still refuse to try new foods.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some children now want to help with cooking at home.`,answer:!0},{text:`Cooking lessons are cheap for schools to organise.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-039`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people can study 'for free', apps 'are good for learning vocabulary', they 'rarely give learners enough chance to speak', and combining them with other activities gives 'much faster progress'.`,passage:`Language apps have made it possible for millions of people to study a foreign language for free on their phones. The apps use games, points and daily reminders to encourage users to practise for a few minutes every day. Many users say this approach helps them build a habit. However, language teachers point out that apps are good for learning vocabulary and basic grammar, but they rarely give learners enough chance to speak or to understand natural conversation. Research suggests that people who combine an app with other activities, such as watching films, reading or talking to native speakers, make much faster progress. The most successful learners, according to one study, were those who used the language in real situations as early as possible.`,statements:[{text:`Most language apps charge users a high monthly fee.`,answer:!1},{text:`Teachers say apps are good for learning vocabulary.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to teachers, apps give plenty of speaking practice.`,answer:!1},{text:`Combining apps with other activities leads to faster progress.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-040`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Petra 'was carved directly into pink sandstone cliffs', the Nabataeans 'became rich by controlling trade routes', the traveller 'had to disguise himself', and authorities 'are considering limiting the number of daily tickets'.`,passage:`The ancient city of Petra, in Jordan, was carved directly into pink sandstone cliffs more than two thousand years ago. It was built by the Nabataeans, a people who became rich by controlling trade routes for spices and incense. For centuries after the city was abandoned, it was known only to local tribes. It was rediscovered by a European traveller in 1812, who had to disguise himself to enter. Today, Petra is Jordan's most popular tourist attraction and was named one of the new Seven Wonders of the World in 2007. However, the large number of visitors is causing damage to the soft stone, and the authorities are considering limiting the number of daily tickets to protect the site.`,statements:[{text:`Petra was carved into the rock.`,answer:!0},{text:`The Nabataeans became wealthy through trade.`,answer:!0},{text:`The European traveller entered Petra in 1812 without any disguise.`,answer:!1},{text:`The authorities are thinking about limiting visitor numbers.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-041`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says laughing 'reduces stress hormones', children 'feel less anxious', members 'start by pretending to laugh' for 'no particular reason', and 'it is hard to measure' the effect.`,passage:`Laughter, it seems, really can be good medicine. Studies have found that laughing reduces stress hormones, relaxes the muscles and may even help the heart. In some hospitals, clowns visit children's wards to cheer up young patients, and doctors report that the children often feel less anxious before operations. There are also laughter yoga clubs, where people meet to laugh together for no particular reason. Members start by pretending to laugh, but the fake laughter usually turns into real laughter quite quickly, as it is very contagious. Researchers admit that it is hard to measure exactly how much laughter improves health, since people who laugh a lot may also have other healthy habits, like exercising more or having strong friendships.`,statements:[{text:`Laughing can help to reduce stress.`,answer:!0},{text:`Hospital clowns make children more anxious before operations.`,answer:!1},{text:`In laughter yoga, people begin by laughing at jokes.`,answer:!1},{text:`It is easy to measure exactly how laughter affects health.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-042`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says there are 'not enough' maths and science teachers, some governments are 'paying off student loans', people train 'while working in a school', and the main reasons are 'heavy workloads and a lack of respect, rather than pay'.`,passage:`In many countries, there are not enough people training to become teachers, especially in subjects such as maths and science. Some governments have tried to solve this by offering higher starting salaries or paying off student loans for people who agree to teach for a certain number of years. Others have created programmes that allow people from other careers, such as engineers or scientists, to train as teachers while working in a school. These programmes have brought many talented people into the profession, but critics say that some new teachers are not given enough support and leave after only a year or two. Surveys show that the main reasons teachers give for leaving are heavy workloads and a lack of respect, rather than pay.`,statements:[{text:`There is a shortage of maths and science teachers in many countries.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some governments pay off student loans for new teachers.`,answer:!0},{text:`Career-change programmes require people to stop working while they train.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most teachers who leave say pay is their main reason.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-043`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they travelled 'by bus', it was 'impossible to follow it exactly', 'a local family invited them to a wedding', and they 'visited fewer places than they had intended'.`,passage:`Lucy and her brother Mark spent last summer travelling around South America by bus. They had planned their route carefully, but they soon realised that it was impossible to follow it exactly. Buses were often delayed, and in Bolivia a road was blocked for two days because of a landslide. Instead of getting annoyed, they decided to stay in the small town where they were stuck, and it turned out to be one of the highlights of the trip. A local family invited them to a wedding, where they danced until three in the morning. By the end of the trip, they had visited fewer places than they had intended, but they both agreed it had been the best summer of their lives.`,statements:[{text:`Lucy and Mark travelled around South America by train.`,answer:!1},{text:`They were able to follow their planned route exactly.`,answer:!1},{text:`They were invited to a wedding by a local family.`,answer:!0},{text:`They visited more places than they had planned.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-044`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'rules differ from one town to another', whole loads 'can be contaminated', reducing waste 'is more effective than recycling', and charging 'has led to a significant fall in waste'.`,passage:`Most people know that recycling helps the environment, but many are confused about what can actually be recycled. Rules differ from one town to another, and a plastic tray that can be recycled in one city may have to go in the general rubbish in the next. When people put the wrong items in recycling bins, whole loads can be contaminated and end up being burned or buried. Experts say that reducing waste in the first place is more effective than recycling. This means buying fewer products with unnecessary packaging, repairing things instead of replacing them and using refillable bottles and bags. Some cities have started charging households for the amount of general rubbish they produce, which has led to a significant fall in waste.`,statements:[{text:`Recycling rules are the same in every town.`,answer:!1},{text:`Putting the wrong items in recycling bins can spoil whole loads.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts think recycling is more effective than reducing waste.`,answer:!1},{text:`Charging households for rubbish has helped to reduce waste in some cities.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-045`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Webb took 'almost twenty-two hours', 'only a small proportion have succeeded', currents mean swimmers 'cover much more', and wetsuits are not allowed for official crossings.`,passage:`The first person to swim across the English Channel was Matthew Webb, a British sailor, who completed the crossing in 1875. It took him almost twenty-two hours, and he covered his body in oil to protect himself from the cold water. Since then, thousands of people have attempted the swim, but only a small proportion have succeeded. The straight-line distance is about thirty-four kilometres, but strong currents mean that swimmers usually cover much more. Swimmers are not allowed to wear wetsuits if they want their crossing to be officially recognised; they can only wear a normal swimming costume, a cap and goggles. Every swimmer must also be accompanied by a boat, which guides them and checks their safety.`,statements:[{text:`Matthew Webb took less than twelve hours to cross the Channel.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most people who try to swim the Channel succeed.`,answer:!1},{text:`Swimmers usually swim further than the straight-line distance.`,answer:!0},{text:`Wetsuits are not allowed for an officially recognised crossing.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-046`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says some customers 'order several sizes', items 'are sometimes simply thrown away', some retailers 'have recently started charging a small fee', and virtual fitting rooms help people 'choose the right size'.`,passage:`Online shopping has transformed the way people buy goods, but it has also created a huge increase in returns. Because many shops offer free returns, some customers order several sizes of the same item and send back the ones that do not fit. This is convenient for shoppers, but it is expensive for companies and bad for the environment, as returned items have to be transported again and are sometimes simply thrown away. A few large retailers have recently started charging a small fee for returns, hoping that customers will think more carefully before they buy. Others are using technology, such as virtual fitting rooms, to help people choose the right size the first time.`,statements:[{text:`Some customers order several sizes of the same item.`,answer:!0},{text:`Returned items are never thrown away.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some retailers have started charging customers for returns.`,answer:!0},{text:`Virtual fitting rooms are designed to help customers choose the correct size.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-047`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she made them 'as a hobby', she got 'over five hundred orders in a single week' after the video, managing money and staff 'has been the hardest part', and she 'has turned down offers'.`,passage:`Grace was only nineteen when she started her own business making candles in her parents' garage. She had been making them as a hobby for a couple of years, and her friends kept asking her to sell them. At first, she sold them at local markets, but after a video she posted online went viral, she received over five hundred orders in a single week. She had to ask her whole family to help her pack the parcels. Two years later, the business has moved into a small workshop and employs four people. Grace says the hardest part has been learning to manage money and staff, which she had never done before. She has turned down offers from larger companies who wanted to buy her business.`,statements:[{text:`Grace started making candles as a hobby.`,answer:!0},{text:`Her online video led to a large number of orders.`,answer:!0},{text:`Grace found managing money and staff easy.`,answer:!1},{text:`Grace sold her business to a larger company.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-048`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says over eighty per cent 'can no longer see the Milky Way', lights 'confuse birds', light 'can disturb people's sleep', and it is 'one of the easiest forms of pollution to reduce'.`,passage:`Light pollution, the excess artificial light produced by cities, is becoming a serious problem. It is estimated that more than eighty per cent of the world's population lives under skies so bright that they can no longer see the Milky Way. But the effects go beyond spoiling the view of the stars. Bright lights at night confuse birds that migrate in the dark, and they attract insects, many of which die as a result. Some studies also suggest that exposure to light at night can disturb people's sleep. Fortunately, light pollution is one of the easiest forms of pollution to reduce. Using lights that point downwards, switching them off when they are not needed and choosing warmer colours can make a big difference.`,statements:[{text:`Most people in the world can still see the Milky Way from where they live.`,answer:!1},{text:`Artificial light at night can confuse migrating birds.`,answer:!0},{text:`Light at night may affect people's sleep.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to the text, light pollution is very difficult to reduce.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-049`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says chess 'was dominated by players from the Soviet Union', top players now 'come from all over the world', players reach a high standard 'at a much younger age', and opinions about creativity differ.`,passage:`For most of the twentieth century, chess was dominated by players from the Soviet Union. Today, however, top players come from all over the world, including India, Norway, China and the United States. One reason for this change is the internet. Young players can now play thousands of games online against opponents of every level, and they can study using powerful computer programs that are stronger than any human. As a result, players are reaching a very high standard at a much younger age than before. Some critics feel that computers have made the game less creative, as players often memorise long sequences of moves. Others argue that the computer has actually revealed new and surprising ideas.`,statements:[{text:`Soviet players dominated chess for much of the twentieth century.`,answer:!0},{text:`Today top chess players come only from a few countries.`,answer:!1},{text:`Players are now reaching a high level at a younger age.`,answer:!0},{text:`Everyone agrees that computers have made chess less creative.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-050`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they are 'cheaper to build and heat', some owners can 'move their home', finding legal land 'is also a challenge', and several owners 'moved back to a normal house'.`,passage:`Many people dream of living in a tiny house: a small, often movable home with just enough space for a bed, a kitchen and a bathroom. Supporters say tiny houses are cheaper to build and heat, and that they encourage people to own fewer possessions. Some owners also enjoy the freedom of being able to move their home to a new location. However, living in such a small space is not always easy, especially for families or couples who work from home. In many countries, finding legal land to park a tiny house is also a challenge, as planning laws were not designed for this type of home. Several owners interviewed for a recent report said they had moved back to a normal house after a few years.`,statements:[{text:`Tiny houses are usually more expensive to heat than normal houses.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some tiny houses can be moved to different places.`,answer:!0},{text:`Finding legal land for a tiny house can be difficult.`,answer:!0},{text:`All the owners interviewed still live in tiny houses.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-051`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the lights are 'caused by particles from the sun', the best time is 'between September and March', seeing them 'is never guaranteed', and companies offer 'a second trip for free'.`,passage:`Every year, millions of people travel to see the northern lights, the colourful lights that appear in the sky near the Arctic. They are caused by particles from the sun colliding with gases in the Earth's atmosphere. The best time to see them is during the long, dark nights between September and March, in places such as northern Norway, Finland, Iceland and Canada. However, seeing them is never guaranteed. A clear sky is essential, and the strength of the lights depends on activity on the sun, which changes from day to day. Tour companies often offer to take visitors to a second trip for free if they do not see the lights the first time. Photographers recommend using a tripod and staying out for several hours.`,statements:[{text:`The northern lights are caused by particles from the sun.`,answer:!0},{text:`The best time to see them is during the summer months.`,answer:!1},{text:`Visitors are always certain to see the lights.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some tour companies offer a free second trip.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-052`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says he accepted 'without thinking twice', most of his day was 'spent in meetings', his boss 'suggested a compromise' of three days and two days, and the company 'has since introduced the same option'.`,passage:`Jake had worked as a software developer for eight years when he was offered a promotion to team manager. The salary was much higher, so he accepted without thinking twice. Within a few months, however, he realised he missed writing code. Most of his day was now spent in meetings, writing reports and dealing with problems between team members. He spoke to his boss, who suggested a compromise: Jake would spend three days a week managing the team and two days working on technical projects. The arrangement has worked well, and Jake says he feels much happier. His company has since introduced the same option for other managers, recognising that not everyone who is good at their job wants to stop doing it.`,statements:[{text:`Jake took a long time to decide whether to accept the promotion.`,answer:!1},{text:`As a manager, Jake spent most of his time in meetings.`,answer:!0},{text:`Jake's boss suggested dividing his week between two roles.`,answer:!0},{text:`The company now offers the same arrangement to other managers.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-053`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the reef 'can even be seen from space', corals 'can recover' if temperatures return to normal, bleaching happens when corals are 'stressed by heat', and reducing emissions 'is the most important step'.`,passage:`The Great Barrier Reef, off the coast of Australia, is the largest coral reef system in the world and can even be seen from space. It is home to thousands of species of fish, as well as turtles, sharks and dolphins. In recent years, however, rising sea temperatures have caused several mass bleaching events. Bleaching happens when corals become stressed by heat and lose the tiny algae that give them their colour and much of their food. If temperatures return to normal quickly, the corals can recover, but if the heat continues for too long, they die. Scientists are experimenting with ways to help, such as breeding corals that can tolerate warmer water, but they agree that reducing global emissions is the most important step.`,statements:[{text:`The Great Barrier Reef is visible from space.`,answer:!0},{text:`Corals always die after bleaching.`,answer:!1},{text:`Bleaching is caused by water that is too cold.`,answer:!1},{text:`Scientists say reducing emissions is the most important solution.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-054`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'the picture is more complicated' and experiments showed 'little difference', 'what you eat matters more than when', eggs and porridge 'keep you full for longer', and experts 'still recommend a healthy breakfast' for children.`,passage:`Breakfast has long been called the most important meal of the day, but recent research suggests the picture is more complicated. Some studies found that people who eat breakfast tend to be slimmer, but this may be because they also have other healthy habits. In controlled experiments, where people were told either to eat breakfast or to skip it, there was little difference in weight after several months. Nutritionists now say that what you eat matters more than when you eat it. A breakfast of sugary cereal or pastries may leave you hungry again by mid-morning, while eggs, yoghurt or porridge can keep you full for longer. For children, however, most experts still recommend a healthy breakfast, as it seems to help concentration at school.`,statements:[{text:`Research clearly proves that breakfast helps people lose weight.`,answer:!1},{text:`Nutritionists think the type of food is more important than the timing.`,answer:!0},{text:`Sugary cereal keeps people full for longer than porridge.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most experts still recommend breakfast for children.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-055`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says napping in Japan is 'not usually seen as a sign of laziness', elsewhere a worker 'might be told off', some firms 'have installed special sleeping pods', and longer sleep 'may leave people feeling worse'.`,passage:`In Japan, it is common for people to take a short nap at their desks or on trains, and this is not usually seen as a sign of laziness. In fact, some people believe that sleeping at work shows that a person is so dedicated that they are exhausted. In other countries, attitudes are very different, and an employee caught sleeping at their desk might be told off by their manager. However, more and more companies around the world are recognising the benefits of short naps. Some technology firms have installed special sleeping pods where staff can rest for twenty minutes. Researchers say a nap of this length can improve alertness and performance, but sleeping for longer may leave people feeling worse.`,statements:[{text:`In Japan, napping at work is usually considered lazy.`,answer:!1},{text:`In some countries, a worker might be criticised for sleeping at their desk.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some companies have installed pods where staff can sleep.`,answer:!0},{text:`Researchers say long naps are better than short ones.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-056`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the market dates from 'the fourteenth century', it 'had shrunk to just a handful of stalls', 'a group of young residents' saved it, and it 'now has over sixty stalls'.`,passage:`The town of Greystone has had a weekly market in its main square since the fourteenth century. For hundreds of years, farmers came from the surrounding villages to sell vegetables, cheese and animals. By the early 2000s, however, the market had shrunk to just a handful of stalls, as most people preferred to shop in supermarkets. A group of young residents decided to try to save it. They invited local producers, artists and street food sellers, and organised live music every Saturday. The changes were a huge success, and the market now has over sixty stalls. Some older residents feel it has become too focused on tourists, but most agree that the square is livelier than it has been for decades.`,statements:[{text:`The market in Greystone is less than a hundred years old.`,answer:!1},{text:`By the early 2000s the market had very few stalls.`,answer:!0},{text:`Older residents organised the changes to the market.`,answer:!1},{text:`The market now has more than sixty stalls.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-057`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says antibiotics were 'first used in the 1940s', colds and flu are caused by viruses 'which antibiotics cannot treat', antibiotics are 'given to farm animals', and simple infections 'could once again become deadly'.`,passage:`Antibiotics have saved millions of lives since they were first used in the 1940s. However, doctors are increasingly worried about antibiotic resistance, which happens when bacteria change and the medicines stop working against them. One of the main causes is the overuse of antibiotics. Many people take them for colds and flu, even though these illnesses are caused by viruses, which antibiotics cannot treat. In some countries, antibiotics are also given to farm animals to make them grow faster. Health organisations are asking doctors to prescribe antibiotics only when they are really needed and are encouraging patients to finish every course they are prescribed. Without action, experts warn, simple infections could once again become deadly.`,statements:[{text:`Antibiotics have been used since the 1940s.`,answer:!0},{text:`Antibiotics are effective against colds and flu.`,answer:!1},{text:`In some countries, farm animals are given antibiotics.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts say simple infections could become dangerous again.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-058`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she 'had never been good at saving money', she 'was shocked' by her spending, the transfer happened 'on the day she was paid', and she 'had saved enough for a short holiday'.`,passage:`Nina had never been good at saving money. Every month, her salary seemed to disappear before she knew where it had gone. After she found herself unable to pay for a car repair, she decided something had to change. She started writing down everything she spent, and she was shocked to discover that she was spending almost two hundred euros a month on takeaway food and coffee. She began cooking at home and taking a flask of coffee to work. She also set up an automatic transfer so that a fixed amount went into a savings account on the day she was paid. A year later, she had saved enough for a short holiday and still had money left for emergencies.`,statements:[{text:`Nina had always been careful with her money.`,answer:!1},{text:`Nina was surprised by how much she spent on takeaway food and coffee.`,answer:!0},{text:`Money was moved into her savings account at the end of each month.`,answer:!1},{text:`After a year Nina could afford a holiday.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-059`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Naismith was 'a Canadian teacher', he wanted a game to play 'indoors', someone had to 'climb a ladder to get the ball out', and players 'had to pass it from where they stood'.`,passage:`Basketball was invented in 1891 by James Naismith, a Canadian teacher working in the United States. He wanted to create a game that his students could play indoors during the cold winter months. The first baskets were actually peach baskets, and every time a player scored, someone had to climb a ladder to get the ball out. It was several years before someone thought of cutting a hole in the bottom. The original rules did not allow players to run with the ball; they had to pass it from where they stood. Since then, the game has spread around the world and is now one of the most popular sports, especially in the United States, China and parts of Europe.`,statements:[{text:`Basketball was invented by an American teacher.`,answer:!1},{text:`Naismith wanted a game that could be played indoors.`,answer:!0},{text:`The first baskets had holes in the bottom.`,answer:!1},{text:`In the original rules, players could not run with the ball.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-060`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says trees 'can lower temperatures in streets', they reduce 'the risk of flooding', people 'wrote love letters and poems', and the aim is to go 'from around twenty per cent to forty per cent'.`,passage:`Many cities are planting more trees to help them cope with hotter summers. Trees provide shade and release water into the air, which can lower temperatures in streets by several degrees. They also absorb rainwater, reducing the risk of flooding, and improve air quality. In Melbourne, the city council has created an online map showing every tree in the city, with information about its age and type. Residents were invited to send emails to individual trees to report problems, but to the council's surprise, thousands of people wrote love letters and poems to their favourite trees instead. The council's aim is to increase the area of the city covered by trees from around twenty per cent to forty per cent.`,statements:[{text:`Trees can help to reduce temperatures in city streets.`,answer:!0},{text:`Trees increase the risk of flooding.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many people in Melbourne sent emails to trees that were not about problems.`,answer:!0},{text:`Melbourne plans to double its tree cover.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-061`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people can buy outfits 'more often than ever before', the industry 'uses huge amounts of water', some brands 'have also started repair services', and critics say the efforts 'are small'.`,passage:`Fast fashion, the production of cheap, trendy clothes in large quantities, has made it possible for people to buy new outfits more often than ever before. However, the environmental cost is enormous. The fashion industry uses huge amounts of water and is responsible for a significant share of global carbon emissions. Many cheap clothes are worn only a few times before being thrown away. In response, a growing number of consumers are turning to second-hand shops, clothing rental services and clothes-swapping events. Some brands have also started repair services or offer discounts to customers who return old clothes for recycling. Critics argue, though, that these efforts are small compared with the number of new garments still being produced every year.`,statements:[{text:`Fast fashion allows people to buy new clothes more frequently.`,answer:!0},{text:`The fashion industry uses very little water.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some brands offer repair services.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics believe brands have already solved the problem.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-062`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she 'found the advice difficult to follow', members 'shared recipes', she walks 'every morning', and 'the support of others made all the difference'.`,passage:`When Priya was diagnosed with diabetes at the age of thirty, she was frightened and confused. Her doctor explained that she would need to change her diet and take more exercise, but she found the advice difficult to follow. Things started to improve when she joined an online community of people with the same condition. Members shared recipes, gave each other tips on managing blood sugar and offered support on bad days. Inspired by them, Priya began walking for an hour every morning and learned to cook meals with less sugar. Two years later, her condition is well controlled, and she now runs a blog with healthy recipes. She says the support of others made all the difference.`,statements:[{text:`Priya found it easy to follow her doctor's advice at first.`,answer:!1},{text:`Members of the online community shared recipes.`,answer:!0},{text:`Priya now walks for an hour every evening.`,answer:!1},{text:`Priya believes support from other people was very important.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-063`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they are 'still far from common', 'most crashes are caused by human error', the technology 'struggles' in heavy snow, and 'it is not clear who should be responsible'.`,passage:`Self-driving cars have been tested on public roads for several years, but they are still far from common. Supporters say they could reduce accidents, since most crashes are caused by human error, such as tiredness or using a phone while driving. They could also give more independence to people who cannot drive, including elderly and disabled people. However, the technology still struggles in certain situations, such as heavy snow, roadworks or busy city centres with unpredictable pedestrians. There are also legal questions: if a self-driving car causes an accident, it is not clear who should be responsible, the owner or the manufacturer. Most experts believe that fully self-driving cars will not be widespread for at least another decade.`,statements:[{text:`Self-driving cars are already common on most roads.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most car accidents are caused by human mistakes.`,answer:!0},{text:`The technology works perfectly in heavy snow.`,answer:!1},{text:`It is unclear who is responsible when a self-driving car causes an accident.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-064`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says there are 'cheap tickets for young people', some performances are 'shown live in cinemas', operas have been staged in 'swimming pools', and most companies 'still depend on government money'.`,passage:`Opera is often seen as an expensive form of entertainment for older and wealthy audiences. To change this image, several opera houses have started offering cheap tickets for young people. In London, people under twenty-five can buy some seats for as little as ten pounds, and certain performances are shown live in cinemas around the world. Some companies have also staged operas in unusual places, such as car parks, swimming pools and old factories, to attract people who would never enter a traditional theatre. These experiments have had some success, with audiences becoming younger and more varied. However, opera remains expensive to produce, since it requires large orchestras, singers and elaborate costumes, so most companies still depend on government money and donations.`,statements:[{text:`Some opera houses offer cheap tickets for young people.`,answer:!0},{text:`Opera performances are never shown in cinemas.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some operas have been performed in swimming pools.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most opera companies no longer need government money.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-065`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says roads 'became impassable' in the rainy season, orders are placed 'by text message', the project 'began in Rwanda', and drones fly 'except during very strong storms'.`,passage:`In some rural parts of Africa, getting medical supplies to remote clinics used to take hours or even days, especially during the rainy season when roads became impassable. Now, drones are being used to deliver blood, vaccines and medicines within minutes. Health workers place an order by text message, and the supplies are dropped by parachute near the clinic. The project began in Rwanda in 2016 and has since expanded to several other countries. Doctors say the faster deliveries have helped save the lives of many mothers who lost a lot of blood after giving birth. The drones are launched from a central base and can fly more than a hundred kilometres in any weather, except during very strong storms.`,statements:[{text:`Roads to some clinics can become impossible to use in the rainy season.`,answer:!0},{text:`Health workers order supplies by email.`,answer:!1},{text:`The drone project started in Rwanda.`,answer:!0},{text:`The drones can fly in very strong storms.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-066`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says tourists are 'far higher than the number of residents', the population 'has fallen sharply', there is 'an entry fee for day visitors on the busiest days', and some residents think the measures 'do not go far enough'.`,passage:`For centuries, Venice has attracted visitors with its canals, bridges and beautiful buildings. In recent years, however, the city has struggled with overtourism. On busy days, the number of tourists is far higher than the number of residents, and narrow streets become so crowded that it is difficult to walk. The local population has fallen sharply, as many residents have moved to the mainland because of high rents and the loss of ordinary shops. To manage the situation, the city introduced an entry fee for day visitors on the busiest days and banned large cruise ships from entering the historic centre. Some residents believe the measures do not go far enough, while some business owners fear they will lose income.`,statements:[{text:`On busy days there are more tourists than residents in Venice.`,answer:!0},{text:`The number of people living in Venice has increased.`,answer:!1},{text:`Day visitors must pay a fee on some days.`,answer:!0},{text:`All residents think the new measures are too strict.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-067`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says some researchers argue 'games are not always harmful', games can improve 'the ability to work in a team', parents should 'occasionally play with them', and consoles should be kept 'out of bedrooms'.`,passage:`Many parents worry that their children spend too much time playing video games. However, some researchers argue that games are not always harmful. Certain games can improve problem-solving skills, hand-eye coordination and the ability to work in a team. Some schools even use games to teach subjects like history and maths. The key, according to experts, is balance. Children who play for several hours every day may sleep less, do less exercise and spend less time with friends in person. Experts suggest that parents should know which games their children are playing, set clear limits and occasionally play with them. They also advise keeping games consoles out of bedrooms, so that playing time is easier to control.`,statements:[{text:`Researchers agree that all video games are harmful.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some games can help children work in a team.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts suggest parents should sometimes play games with their children.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts recommend keeping consoles in children's bedrooms.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-068`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says he was 'more interested in cooking the fish than catching it', he trained 'in Milan', 'the menu changes daily', and he 'refuses to serve species that are in danger'.`,passage:`Marco grew up in a family of fishermen on the coast of Sicily, but as a teenager he was more interested in cooking the fish than catching it. After finishing school, he moved to Milan to train as a chef, working in several famous restaurants. Ten years later, he returned to his hometown and opened a small restaurant by the harbour. Every morning he buys fish directly from the boats, including from his own father, and the menu changes daily depending on what has been caught. He refuses to serve species that are in danger from overfishing, even when customers ask for them. Last year the restaurant was included in a well-known food guide, and it is now usually necessary to book weeks in advance.`,statements:[{text:`As a teenager, Marco wanted to become a fisherman.`,answer:!1},{text:`Marco trained as a chef in Milan.`,answer:!0},{text:`The menu at Marco's restaurant is the same every day.`,answer:!1},{text:`Marco will not serve fish that are threatened by overfishing.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-069`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says conversations 'are more relaxed', walking 'can increase creative thinking', they are 'not suitable for every situation', and they work best 'for small groups of two or three people'.`,passage:`Walking meetings, in which colleagues discuss work while taking a walk instead of sitting in an office, are becoming more popular. People who have tried them say they feel more creative and that conversations are more relaxed, partly because they are walking side by side rather than facing each other across a table. Studies have shown that walking can increase creative thinking, at least for a short time afterwards. Walking meetings also help people who spend most of the day sitting down to move more. However, they are not suitable for every situation. They work best for small groups of two or three people and are not ideal when notes need to be taken or documents need to be looked at.`,statements:[{text:`People say walking meetings feel more relaxed.`,answer:!0},{text:`Studies show that walking may increase creative thinking.`,answer:!0},{text:`Walking meetings are suitable for every kind of meeting.`,answer:!1},{text:`Walking meetings work best with large groups.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-070`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the trees 'absorb huge amounts of carbon dioxide', many species 'have not yet been studied', forest has been cut 'to make room for cattle farms', and deforestation is 'much lower' in indigenous areas.`,passage:`The Amazon rainforest is often called the lungs of the planet because its trees absorb huge amounts of carbon dioxide. It is also home to around ten per cent of all known species on Earth, many of which have not yet been studied by scientists. However, large areas of the forest have been cut down to make room for cattle farms and crops such as soya. Some scientists warn that if deforestation continues, parts of the Amazon could turn into dry grassland, releasing carbon instead of storing it. Indigenous communities, who have lived in the forest for thousands of years, play an important role in protecting it, and studies show that deforestation rates are much lower in areas they control.`,statements:[{text:`The Amazon's trees absorb a lot of carbon dioxide.`,answer:!0},{text:`All species in the Amazon have already been studied.`,answer:!1},{text:`Forest has been cleared to make space for cattle farms.`,answer:!0},{text:`Deforestation is higher in areas controlled by indigenous communities.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-071`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the school 'hardly gives any' homework, pupils 'perform very well in national tests', there is 'a fifteen-minute break after every lesson', and the methods 'might not work everywhere'.`,passage:`A school in Finland has become famous for its unusual approach to homework: it hardly gives any. Teachers believe that children learn best during the school day and that evenings should be for family, sport and play. Despite this, the school's pupils perform very well in national tests. The head teacher explains that lessons are carefully planned and that classes are small, so teachers can give individual attention to each pupil. Pupils also have a fifteen-minute break after every lesson, which teachers say helps them concentrate. Visitors from many countries have come to observe the school, but the head teacher warns that its methods might not work everywhere, as they depend on well-trained teachers and strong public trust in schools.`,statements:[{text:`The school gives pupils a lot of homework.`,answer:!1},{text:`Pupils at the school do well in national tests.`,answer:!0},{text:`Pupils have a short break after every lesson.`,answer:!0},{text:`The head teacher believes the methods would work in any country.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-072`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they spent 'only about fifteen minutes at the top', the climb 'would have been impossible without Tenzing', they 'refused to answer', and Hillary built 'schools and hospitals' in Nepal.`,passage:`In 1953, Edmund Hillary from New Zealand and Tenzing Norgay, a Sherpa from Nepal, became the first people confirmed to have reached the summit of Mount Everest. They spent only about fifteen minutes at the top, taking photographs and leaving small gifts in the snow. Hillary later said that the climb would have been impossible without Tenzing's experience and strength. For years, journalists kept asking which of them had stepped onto the summit first, but the two men refused to answer, saying they had reached it together as a team. After the expedition, Hillary spent much of his life helping the Sherpa people, building schools and hospitals in the mountain villages of Nepal.`,statements:[{text:`Hillary and Tenzing stayed at the summit for several hours.`,answer:!1},{text:`Hillary believed Tenzing's help was essential to the climb.`,answer:!0},{text:`The two men told journalists which of them reached the summit first.`,answer:!1},{text:`Hillary later built schools in Nepal.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-073`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says sales 'have risen steadily', there are 'cafés where customers can pay to play', some games 'take several hours to complete', and fans 'pay in advance'.`,passage:`Board games, which many people thought would disappear in the age of video games, have enjoyed a remarkable comeback. Sales have risen steadily over the past decade, and cafés where customers can pay to play from a large collection of games have opened in cities around the world. Many players say they enjoy the chance to spend time with friends face to face, away from screens. Modern board games are also very different from the classic family games of the past. Many involve strategy, cooperation and complex stories, and some take several hours to complete. Designers often raise money for new games through crowdfunding websites, where fans pay in advance to help produce games they would like to see made.`,statements:[{text:`Board game sales have fallen in the last ten years.`,answer:!1},{text:`There are cafés where people pay to play board games.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some modern board games take several hours to finish.`,answer:!0},{text:`Fans sometimes pay in advance to help produce new games.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-074`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'water rose so quickly' that people were rescued by boat, volunteers 'came from nearby towns', the restaurant 'cooked free meals', and last winter 'no homes were flooded'.`,passage:`When a severe flood hit the town of Ashford Bridge two winters ago, dozens of homes and shops were badly damaged. Water rose so quickly that many residents had to be rescued by boat. In the weeks that followed, hundreds of volunteers came from nearby towns to help clean houses and remove ruined furniture. A local restaurant cooked free meals every day for families who had lost their kitchens. The disaster also led to changes: the council built new flood barriers along the river and planted trees on the hills above the town to slow down rainwater. Last winter, when heavy rain fell again, the barriers held, and no homes were flooded. Residents say the experience brought the community closer together.`,statements:[{text:`The water rose slowly, giving residents plenty of time to leave.`,answer:!1},{text:`Volunteers from other towns helped after the flood.`,answer:!0},{text:`A restaurant charged families for meals after the flood.`,answer:!1},{text:`No homes were flooded during last winter's heavy rain.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-075`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says spices 'were traded like gold', 'only the wealthy could afford them', spices were 'one of the main reasons for the great voyages', and saffron is 'one of the most expensive foods'.`,passage:`Spices were once so valuable that they were traded like gold. In the Middle Ages, pepper, cinnamon and nutmeg were brought to Europe from Asia along long and dangerous routes, and their price increased greatly at every stage of the journey. Only the wealthy could afford them, and serving dishes full of spices was a way of showing off one's wealth. The desire to find a direct sea route to the countries where spices grew was one of the main reasons for the great voyages of exploration in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. Today, spices are cheap and available in every supermarket, but saffron, which is collected by hand from flowers, is still one of the most expensive foods in the world.`,statements:[{text:`Spices were once very valuable.`,answer:!0},{text:`In the Middle Ages, spices were cheap for ordinary people.`,answer:!1},{text:`The search for spices encouraged voyages of exploration.`,answer:!0},{text:`Saffron is now one of the cheapest spices.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-076`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'fewer people borrow printed books', some libraries 'lend unusual items such as tools, musical instruments', librarians 'spend much of their time helping people with technology', and some 'reduce opening hours'.`,passage:`Public libraries have had to reinvent themselves in the digital age. Fewer people borrow printed books than in the past, but many libraries are busier than ever because they now offer much more. Visitors can use computers and free Wi-Fi, attend language classes, borrow e-books and even use 3D printers. Some libraries lend unusual items such as tools, musical instruments and board games. For many people who cannot afford internet access at home, the library is the only place where they can apply for jobs online or contact government services. Librarians, therefore, spend much of their time helping people with technology. Despite their importance, many libraries have faced budget cuts, and some have been forced to reduce opening hours or close altogether.`,statements:[{text:`Fewer people borrow printed books than before.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some libraries lend tools and musical instruments.`,answer:!0},{text:`Librarians rarely help people with technology.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some libraries have reduced their opening hours.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-077`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the job involves 'far more waiting than taking pictures', she waited 'eleven days', she is paid 'only when magazines or companies buy her pictures', and she advises studying 'animal behaviour'.`,passage:`Hannah works as a wildlife photographer, a job many people consider a dream. However, she explains that it involves far more waiting than taking pictures. To photograph a rare bird in Scotland last year, she spent eleven days hiding in a small shelter, often in freezing rain, before the bird finally appeared. She also has to spend a lot of time on less exciting tasks, such as editing photos, writing proposals and managing her finances. Income is uncertain, since she is paid only when magazines or companies buy her pictures. Despite the difficulties, Hannah says she cannot imagine doing anything else. Her advice to young people who want to follow the same path is to study animal behaviour, not just photography.`,statements:[{text:`Hannah spends most of her time actually taking photographs.`,answer:!1},{text:`She waited for less than a week to photograph the rare bird.`,answer:!1},{text:`Hannah has a fixed monthly salary.`,answer:!1},{text:`She advises young photographers to learn about animal behaviour.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-078`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says designers want to keep attention 'for as long as possible', guests 'hand in their devices', participants 'feel anxious for the first day or two', and 'many find it hard to keep their new habits'.`,passage:`Most people check their phones dozens of times a day, often without even realising it. App designers use techniques such as notifications, likes and endless scrolling to keep users' attention for as long as possible. In response, a growing number of people are trying a 'digital detox', spending a weekend or even a whole week without their phones. Some hotels and holiday companies now offer detox breaks where guests hand in their devices when they arrive. Participants often say they feel anxious for the first day or two, but then begin to enjoy reading, walking and talking to other people. However, many find it hard to keep their new habits once they return to their normal routine.`,statements:[{text:`App designers try to keep users' attention for a long time.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some hotels ask guests to hand in their phones.`,answer:!0},{text:`Participants usually feel relaxed from the very first day.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most people easily keep their new habits after a detox.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-079`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says historians 'are not sure', the modern Games were 'first held in Athens in 1896', the distance was 'not fixed until 1908', and it was chosen to 'finish in front of the royal family's box'.`,passage:`The marathon gets its name from a Greek legend. According to the story, in 490 BC a messenger ran about forty kilometres from the town of Marathon to Athens to announce that the Greeks had won a battle against the Persians, and then died from exhaustion. Historians are not sure whether the story is true. When the modern Olympic Games were first held in Athens in 1896, organisers created a race following the same route to honour the legend. The distance was not fixed until 1908, when the London Olympics set it at 42.195 kilometres, reportedly so that the race could finish in front of the royal family's box in the stadium.`,statements:[{text:`Historians are certain that the legend of the messenger is true.`,answer:!1},{text:`The modern Olympic Games were first held in Athens.`,answer:!0},{text:`The official marathon distance was fixed in 1896.`,answer:!1},{text:`The 1908 distance was reportedly chosen so the race could finish near the royal box.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-080`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says allergies seem 'more common, especially in wealthy countries', farm children 'are less likely to develop allergies', doctors 'used to advise' avoiding peanuts, and early introduction 'reduces the risk'.`,passage:`Allergies seem to be becoming more common, especially in wealthy countries. One theory, known as the hygiene hypothesis, suggests that children who grow up in very clean environments do not come into contact with enough germs, so their immune systems overreact to harmless things like pollen or peanuts. Studies have found that children who grow up on farms, surrounded by animals, are less likely to develop allergies. For peanut allergy, doctors used to advise parents to avoid giving peanuts to babies. However, recent research showed that introducing small amounts of peanut early in life actually reduces the risk. Parents are now advised to speak to a doctor before doing this, particularly if their child already has other allergies.`,statements:[{text:`Allergies appear to be increasing in wealthy countries.`,answer:!0},{text:`Children who grow up on farms are more likely to have allergies.`,answer:!1},{text:`Doctors have always advised giving peanuts to babies.`,answer:!1},{text:`Introducing peanuts early may reduce the risk of allergy.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-081`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says many people told him 'bookshops had no future', he did not try 'to compete with online stores on price', staff 'write personal recommendations', and he is only 'thinking of opening a second one'.`,passage:`Bruno opened a bookshop in a quiet street in Lisbon at a time when many people told him that bookshops had no future. Instead of trying to compete with online stores on price, he focused on creating a place where people wanted to spend time. The shop has comfortable chairs, a small café and a garden at the back. Bruno and his staff read widely and write personal recommendations on cards placed next to the books. Every week, the shop hosts readings, book clubs and children's storytelling sessions. Customers often say that they discover books there that they would never have found online. Eight years after opening, the shop is profitable, and Bruno is thinking of opening a second one in Porto.`,statements:[{text:`Everyone told Bruno that his bookshop would be a great success.`,answer:!1},{text:`Bruno tried to offer lower prices than online stores.`,answer:!1},{text:`Staff write personal recommendations for books.`,answer:!0},{text:`Bruno has already opened a second shop in Porto.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-082`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says plants 'grow without soil', farms use 'up to ninety-five per cent less water', they 'use large amounts of electricity', and growing wheat this way 'is not yet economical'.`,passage:`Vertical farming is a method of growing crops indoors in stacked layers, often inside old warehouses or purpose-built towers. Plants grow without soil, with their roots in water containing nutrients, and LED lights replace the sun. Supporters say vertical farms use up to ninety-five per cent less water than traditional farms and need no pesticides. Because they can be built in cities, food does not need to travel long distances to reach customers. However, the farms use large amounts of electricity for lighting and temperature control, which makes them expensive to run. At present, they mostly grow salad leaves and herbs, which grow quickly and sell at high prices. Growing staple crops such as wheat or rice this way is not yet economical.`,statements:[{text:`In vertical farms, plants grow in soil.`,answer:!1},{text:`Vertical farms use much less water than traditional farms.`,answer:!0},{text:`Vertical farms are cheap to run because they need little electricity.`,answer:!1},{text:`Growing wheat in vertical farms is currently not economical.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-083`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says this comes 'from a desire to be liked', saying yes 'can lead to stress', experts suggest saying 'you need to check your schedule' instead of answering immediately, and people 'respect colleagues who set clear limits'.`,passage:`Many people find it difficult to say no when colleagues ask for help, even when they are already very busy. According to psychologists, this often comes from a desire to be liked or a fear of seeming lazy. However, always saying yes can lead to stress, long hours and poor-quality work. Experts suggest a few simple strategies. Instead of answering immediately, you can say you need to check your schedule, which gives you time to think. If you do decide to refuse, it helps to be polite but clear and, if possible, to suggest an alternative, such as helping later in the week. Psychologists also point out that people generally respect colleagues who set clear limits.`,statements:[{text:`Some people find it hard to refuse requests because they want to be liked.`,answer:!0},{text:`Always agreeing to help can lead to stress.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts recommend always answering requests immediately.`,answer:!1},{text:`People generally respect colleagues who set clear limits.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-084`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says companies 'have taken paying customers', passengers experience 'a few minutes of weightlessness', 'only the very rich can afford them', and supporters compare it to 'air travel in the early twentieth century'.`,passage:`Space tourism, once only a dream, has started to become a reality. In recent years, several private companies have taken paying customers on short flights to the edge of space, where they experience a few minutes of weightlessness and see the curve of the Earth. Tickets cost hundreds of thousands of dollars, so for now only the very rich can afford them. Supporters argue that, just like air travel in the early twentieth century, prices will eventually fall. Critics, however, point out that rocket launches produce significant pollution and question whether it is right to spend so much money on such trips when there are serious problems on Earth. Some scientists also worry about the growing amount of rubbish orbiting the planet.`,statements:[{text:`Private companies have taken paying customers to the edge of space.`,answer:!0},{text:`Passengers experience several hours of weightlessness.`,answer:!1},{text:`Space flights are currently affordable for most people.`,answer:!1},{text:`Supporters compare space tourism to early air travel.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-085`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says her family 'was shocked', she started baking 'to relax', she took a course 'in Paris', and her mother 'helps out at the counter on busy weekends'.`,passage:`Kim was a successful lawyer in Seoul when she decided to give up her career to become a professional baker. Her family was shocked, as they had spent a lot of money on her education. Kim had started baking to relax after long days at work, and she soon found that she enjoyed it far more than her job. She took a six-month course in Paris, then returned home and opened a small bakery specialising in French pastries with Korean flavours, such as green tea and sweet red beans. At first, her parents refused to visit the shop. Now, however, they are among her most loyal customers, and her mother helps out at the counter on busy weekends.`,statements:[{text:`Kim's family was surprised by her decision.`,answer:!0},{text:`Kim started baking as a way to relax.`,answer:!0},{text:`Kim studied baking in Seoul.`,answer:!1},{text:`Kim's mother sometimes works in the bakery.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-086`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says wolves 'disappeared from many parts of Europe', they appear 'a few kilometres from large cities', experts 'welcome their return', and governments help farmers 'buy electric fences'.`,passage:`Wolves were hunted until they disappeared from many parts of Europe during the nineteenth and twentieth centuries. In recent decades, however, thanks to legal protection, they have returned to countries such as Germany, France and Italy, sometimes appearing just a few kilometres from large cities. Wildlife experts welcome their return, saying that wolves help keep populations of deer and wild boar under control, which allows forests to recover. Farmers, on the other hand, are worried, because wolves sometimes kill sheep and goats. Governments now pay compensation for animals killed by wolves and help farmers buy electric fences and guard dogs. The debate remains heated, and in some areas there have been calls to allow limited hunting again.`,statements:[{text:`Wolves had disappeared from many parts of Europe in the past.`,answer:!0},{text:`Wolves never come close to large cities.`,answer:!1},{text:`Wildlife experts are worried about the return of wolves.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some governments help farmers buy electric fences.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-087`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people 'are living longer', 'over forty per cent of grandparents provide regular childcare', daily childcare 'can be exhausting', and some grandparents 'feel they cannot refuse'.`,passage:`In many countries, people are living longer than ever before. As a result, more and more grandparents are helping to look after their grandchildren while parents work. Surveys suggest that in some European countries, over forty per cent of grandparents provide regular childcare. Many say they enjoy spending time with the children and feel it keeps them active. Some studies have even found that grandparents who help occasionally tend to live longer. However, looking after young children every day can be exhausting, especially for older people with health problems. Some grandparents also feel they cannot refuse, even when they would prefer to travel or pursue their own hobbies during retirement.`,statements:[{text:`People in many countries are living longer.`,answer:!0},{text:`In some European countries, more than forty per cent of grandparents provide regular childcare.`,answer:!0},{text:`Looking after children every day is never tiring for grandparents.`,answer:!1},{text:`All grandparents are happy to provide childcare whenever they are asked.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-088`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the Maya and Aztecs 'drank cacao', beans 'were used as money', bars 'were not produced until the nineteenth century', and most cacao 'is grown in West Africa'.`,passage:`Chocolate comes from the seeds of the cacao tree, which grows in hot, humid regions near the equator. The ancient Maya and Aztecs drank cacao as a bitter, spicy drink, and cacao beans were so valuable that they were used as money. When chocolate arrived in Europe in the sixteenth century, sugar was added to make it sweeter, and it became a fashionable drink among the rich. Solid chocolate bars were not produced until the nineteenth century. Today, most of the world's cacao is grown in West Africa, mainly by small farmers, many of whom earn very little. Some chocolate companies now pay farmers higher prices through fair trade schemes, though critics say much more needs to be done.`,statements:[{text:`The ancient Maya and Aztecs ate chocolate in bars.`,answer:!1},{text:`Cacao beans were once used as money.`,answer:!0},{text:`Solid chocolate bars were first made in the sixteenth century.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most cacao today is grown in West Africa.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-089`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Frank expected 'about twenty people' but 'more than a hundred and fifty turned up', the council agreed 'to close the road', residents 'set up a group chat', and the garden was started 'since then'.`,passage:`Last spring, the residents of Elm Street, a busy road in a large city, decided they were tired of not knowing their neighbours. One of them, a retired teacher called Frank, printed leaflets inviting everyone to a street party. He expected about twenty people to come, but more than a hundred and fifty turned up, bringing food from many different countries. The council agreed to close the road to traffic for the afternoon. Since then, the residents have set up a group chat where they share tools, offer to water each other's plants and warn each other about problems. They have also started a small community garden on an empty piece of land at the end of the street.`,statements:[{text:`Frank expected far more people than actually came.`,answer:!1},{text:`The road was closed to traffic for the party.`,answer:!0},{text:`Residents now use a group chat to help each other.`,answer:!0},{text:`The community garden was created before the street party.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-090`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says dogs 'are descended from wolves', following a pointing finger is 'something even chimpanzees find difficult', both 'produce a hormone linked to love and trust', and dogs detect 'illnesses by smell'.`,passage:`Dogs have lived alongside humans for at least fifteen thousand years, and scientists believe they are descended from wolves that began to stay close to human camps, perhaps to eat leftover food. Over time, humans bred dogs for different purposes, such as hunting, herding sheep and guarding homes. Research has shown that dogs are particularly good at understanding human signals; for example, they can follow a pointing finger, something even chimpanzees find difficult. Studies have also found that when dogs and their owners look into each other's eyes, both produce a hormone linked to love and trust. Today, dogs work in many roles, from guiding blind people to detecting illnesses by smell.`,statements:[{text:`Scientists believe dogs are descended from wolves.`,answer:!0},{text:`Chimpanzees are better than dogs at following a pointing finger.`,answer:!1},{text:`Looking into each other's eyes produces a hormone in both dogs and owners.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some dogs can detect illnesses by smell.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-091`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says video is used 'for the first stage', recorded interviews happen 'without anyone watching live', light should be 'in front of them, not behind', and candidates should dress 'just as you would for a face-to-face interview'.`,passage:`Job interviews are increasingly being carried out by video, especially for the first stage of recruitment. Some companies even use recorded interviews, in which candidates answer questions in front of a camera without anyone watching live. Recruitment experts say that preparing for a video interview requires some extra thought. Candidates should test their camera and microphone in advance, choose a quiet room with a plain background and make sure the light is in front of them, not behind. It is also important to look at the camera rather than at the screen, so that the interviewer feels you are making eye contact. Experts recommend dressing just as you would for a face-to-face interview, even if you are at home.`,statements:[{text:`Video interviews are mainly used for the final stage of recruitment.`,answer:!1},{text:`In recorded interviews, someone is always watching live.`,answer:!1},{text:`Candidates should sit with the light behind them.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts advise dressing as you would for a face-to-face interview.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-092`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Singapore has 'very little land', 'around eighty per cent of residents live in public housing', owning a car is 'extremely expensive', and new buildings must 'include plants and gardens'.`,passage:`Singapore is often held up as an example of a city that has managed rapid growth successfully. Despite having very little land, it has some of the best housing, public transport and green spaces in Asia. Around eighty per cent of residents live in public housing flats, which are built by the government and sold to citizens at affordable prices. To reduce traffic, the city charges drivers for using busy roads at peak times, and owning a car is extremely expensive. The city has also introduced rules requiring new buildings to include plants and gardens, sometimes on their roofs and walls. Critics, however, point out that the government controls many aspects of daily life very strictly.`,statements:[{text:`Singapore has a lot of spare land for building.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most residents live in public housing.`,answer:!0},{text:`Owning a car in Singapore is cheap.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some new buildings must include gardens.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-093`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she stopped 'after a shoulder injury', she 'hardly swam at all' for almost twenty years, she swims 'all year round', and her Swiss swim was 'to raise money for a children's hospital'.`,passage:`Rachel had been a talented swimmer as a child, but she stopped competing at sixteen after a shoulder injury. For almost twenty years she hardly swam at all. Then, at the age of thirty-five, a friend persuaded her to try open-water swimming in a nearby lake. She was nervous at first, as the water was cold and she could not see the bottom, but she soon fell in love with the feeling of freedom. She now swims in the lake all year round, even in winter, when the water temperature can drop to four degrees. Last summer she swam across a ten-kilometre lake in Switzerland to raise money for a children's hospital, and she is now planning to swim the English Channel.`,statements:[{text:`Rachel stopped competing because of an injury.`,answer:!0},{text:`Rachel swam regularly between the ages of sixteen and thirty-five.`,answer:!1},{text:`Rachel only swims in the lake during the summer.`,answer:!1},{text:`Rachel's swim in Switzerland raised money for charity.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-094`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says this is 'partly determined by our genes', larks 'feel most alert in the morning', teenagers 'tend to become owls', and students' 'grades improved' at schools that started later.`,passage:`Most of us have a favourite time of day for working, and scientists say this is partly determined by our genes. Some people, often called 'larks', naturally wake up early and feel most alert in the morning. Others, known as 'owls', prefer to go to bed late and work best in the evening. Teenagers tend to become owls for a few years, which is one reason why many find early school starts so hard. Some schools that moved their start time later found that students were less tired and their grades improved. Because the traditional working day suits larks better, owls may feel tired and less productive, and some experts argue that employers should allow more flexible hours.`,statements:[{text:`Our genes partly influence our favourite time of day for working.`,answer:!0},{text:`Larks feel most alert in the evening.`,answer:!1},{text:`Teenagers often become owls for a few years.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some schools with later start times saw grades improve.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-095`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Medellín was 'one of the most dangerous cities', cable cars connect hillside areas 'with the city's metro system', 'crime has fallen significantly', and 'problems remain, such as inequality'.`,passage:`The city of Medellín, in Colombia, was once known as one of the most dangerous cities in the world. Since the 1990s, however, it has changed dramatically. One of the most important projects was the construction of cable cars connecting poor neighbourhoods on the steep hillsides with the city's metro system. Before this, residents had to walk for hours or take several buses to reach the city centre. The city also built libraries, parks and schools in these areas, often with impressive modern architecture. Crime has fallen significantly, and Medellín has won international awards for innovation. Problems remain, such as inequality and rising house prices, but the city is now a popular destination for tourists.`,statements:[{text:`Medellín was once considered very dangerous.`,answer:!0},{text:`The cable cars connect hillside neighbourhoods with the metro.`,answer:!0},{text:`Crime in Medellín has increased since the 1990s.`,answer:!1},{text:`Medellín no longer has any problems with inequality.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-096`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says programmes include 'protecting turtles on beaches', experts have 'raised concerns', organisations advise 'longer placements', and 'donating money can sometimes be more helpful'.`,passage:`Volunteering abroad has become popular among young people who want to travel and do something useful at the same time. Programmes range from teaching English to building schools or protecting turtles on beaches. However, some experts have raised concerns about so-called 'voluntourism'. They point out that short-term volunteers without proper skills may do work that local people could be paid to do, and that children in orphanages can be harmed when volunteers come and go every few weeks. Organisations that support responsible volunteering advise people to choose longer placements, check that the project is run with local communities and ask whether their skills are actually needed. They also suggest that donating money can sometimes be more helpful than volunteering in person.`,statements:[{text:`Volunteering programmes abroad include protecting turtles.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts say short-term volunteers always help local communities.`,answer:!1},{text:`Responsible organisations recommend choosing shorter placements.`,answer:!1},{text:`Donating money can sometimes be more useful than volunteering.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-097`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says some modern roads 'still follow the same routes', aqueducts used 'only gravity', the concrete 'became stronger over time', and researchers hope to make modern concrete 'more long-lasting'.`,passage:`The ancient Romans were remarkable engineers, and many of their structures are still standing today. Their roads were built in layers of stone and gravel, which made them strong enough to last for centuries, and some modern roads in Europe still follow the same routes. The Romans also built aqueducts to carry water over long distances to their cities, using only gravity. One of their greatest inventions was a type of concrete that has proved extremely durable. Scientists have discovered that Roman concrete used in harbours actually became stronger over time, as seawater reacted with volcanic ash in the mixture. Researchers are now studying it in the hope of making modern concrete more long-lasting and environmentally friendly.`,statements:[{text:`Some modern European roads follow Roman routes.`,answer:!0},{text:`Roman aqueducts used pumps to move water.`,answer:!1},{text:`Roman concrete in harbours became weaker over time.`,answer:!1},{text:`Scientists are studying Roman concrete to improve modern concrete.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-098`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says he had 'months of unsuccessful applications' and retrained, former colleagues 'thought he was making a mistake', the first weeks were 'exhausting', and he is 'particularly good at helping pupils who struggled with maths'.`,passage:`When Leo was made redundant from his job in a bank at the age of fifty-two, he worried that nobody would employ him again. After months of unsuccessful applications, he decided to retrain as a primary school teaching assistant, something he had always been curious about. Many of his former colleagues thought he was making a mistake, since the salary was much lower. Leo admits that the first few weeks were exhausting, as he was not used to the noise and energy of thirty children. However, he soon discovered that his patience and experience with numbers made him particularly good at helping pupils who struggled with maths. He says he has never enjoyed a job so much, even though he earns less.`,statements:[{text:`Leo found a new job in a bank soon after being made redundant.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some former colleagues thought his decision was a mistake.`,answer:!0},{text:`Leo found the first few weeks at school very relaxing.`,answer:!1},{text:`Leo is good at helping pupils who find maths difficult.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-099`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the Arctic tern 'holds the record for the longest migration', scientists are 'still not completely sure', birds seem to use 'the Earth's magnetic field', and many die from 'collisions with buildings, especially brightly lit ones'.`,passage:`Every year, millions of birds migrate thousands of kilometres between their summer and winter homes. The Arctic tern holds the record for the longest migration of any animal, flying from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back again each year, a round trip of around seventy thousand kilometres. Scientists are still not completely sure how birds find their way. They seem to use a combination of the position of the sun and stars, the Earth's magnetic field and familiar landmarks such as rivers and coastlines. Some birds can fly for days without stopping. Migration is extremely dangerous, however, and many birds die from exhaustion, storms or collisions with buildings, especially brightly lit ones at night.`,statements:[{text:`The Arctic tern makes the longest migration of any animal.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientists fully understand how birds find their way.`,answer:!1},{text:`Birds may use the Earth's magnetic field to navigate.`,answer:!0},{text:`Brightly lit buildings are a danger to migrating birds.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-100`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says hockey is 'the national winter sport of Canada', parents drive children to 'early-morning practice', the cost 'has risen sharply', and programmes 'provide free second-hand equipment'.`,passage:`Ice hockey is the national winter sport of Canada, and for many Canadian children, learning to skate is almost as natural as learning to walk. In small towns across the country, the local ice rink is often the centre of community life during the long winters. Parents get up before dawn to drive their children to early-morning practice, and whole towns come out to watch local teams play at weekends. However, the cost of playing hockey has risen sharply. Equipment, ice time and travel to tournaments can cost families thousands of dollars a year, and some worry that the sport is becoming available only to wealthier families. Several programmes now provide free second-hand equipment to children whose families cannot afford it.`,statements:[{text:`Ice hockey is Canada's national winter sport.`,answer:!0},{text:`Hockey practice for children usually takes place late in the evening.`,answer:!1},{text:`The cost of playing hockey has fallen in recent years.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some programmes give free equipment to children from poorer families.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-101`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says balance "is difficult" for adults, instructors recommend "using the feet to push along", and most adults "manage to ride within a few hours".`,passage:`Learning to ride a bicycle as an adult is difficult because balance relies on instincts that are harder to adjust. Instructors recommend starting without pedals, using the feet to push along while sitting on the saddle, so the learner can feel balance without the fear of falling. Once the rider can glide for several metres, pedals are added. Most adults manage to ride within a few hours, and many say the freedom is worth the embarrassment of starting late.`,statements:[{text:`Adults find balance easier to learn than children.`,answer:!1},{text:`Learners may begin by pushing with their feet.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most adults take several weeks to learn.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-102`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she "never used a recipe", cooking is "about paying attention", and the soup "is never exactly the same twice".`,passage:`My grandmother never used a recipe. She would look at what was in the cupboard, taste the sauce as it cooked and add a pinch of something until it was right. When I tried to write down her famous soup, she laughed and said that a recipe would only make me worry about measurements instead of flavour. Years later, I realised she had been teaching me something important. Cooking, she believed, is not about following rules but about paying attention. I still cook that way, and my soup is never exactly the same twice.`,statements:[{text:`The writer's grandmother measured every ingredient carefully.`,answer:!1},{text:`She thought that attention was more important than rules.`,answer:!0},{text:`The writer's soup always tastes identical.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-103`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "some tours are free", others focus on "street art, food markets", and writers recommend a tour "on the first day".`,passage:`Walking tours led by local guides have become popular in many cities. Unlike traditional bus trips, they allow visitors to explore small streets, hear personal stories and ask questions. Some tours are free, and the guides rely on tips at the end. Others focus on specific interests, such as street art, food markets or hidden courtyards. Travel writers recommend taking a walking tour on the first day of a trip, because it helps visitors understand the layout of the city and gives them ideas for the rest of their stay.`,statements:[{text:`Walking tours are always expensive.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some tours concentrate on specific interests such as street art.`,answer:!0},{text:`Travel writers recommend a tour at the end of a trip.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-104`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says accidents are "rising", "a sunny morning can turn into a storm by midday", and "a paper map is still useful".`,passage:`Mountain guides in the Alps say that the number of accidents is rising, mostly because inexperienced walkers underestimate the weather. A sunny morning can turn into a storm by midday, and temperatures at altitude can fall far below zero even in summer. Guides recommend checking the forecast, carrying warm clothes and a first aid kit, and turning back if the weather changes. They also point out that mobile phones do not always work in the mountains, so a paper map is still useful.`,statements:[{text:`The number of mountain accidents is falling.`,answer:!1},{text:`Weather can change quickly in the mountains.`,answer:!0},{text:`Guides say a paper map is no longer useful.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-105`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the tradition "goes back many centuries", couples ask for "a charity", and guests should not "spend more than they can afford".`,passage:`The tradition of giving gifts at weddings goes back many centuries. In some countries, guests give money in an envelope, while in others the couple prepares a list of presents in a shop. Recently, an increasing number of couples have asked guests to contribute to their honeymoon or to a charity. This avoids duplicate presents and unwanted objects, but some older guests feel uncomfortable about giving money. Etiquette experts say that whatever the couple chooses, guests should respect the request and never feel pressured to spend more than they can afford.`,statements:[{text:`Wedding gifts are a very recent tradition.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some couples ask for donations to charity.`,answer:!0},{text:`Etiquette experts say guests should spend as much as possible.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-106`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "she knew nobody", "several friendships faded quickly", and she said yes "even when she felt tired".`,passage:`Moving to a new city alone can be lonely, but it can also be a chance to start afresh. When Lucia arrived in Edinburgh, she knew nobody. She forced herself to say yes to every invitation during the first month, even when she felt tired. Within weeks she had joined a choir, a walking group and a language exchange. She admits that some evenings were awkward and that several friendships faded quickly. Still, three of the people she met in that first month are now her closest friends, and she says the effort was worth it.`,statements:[{text:`Lucia already had friends in Edinburgh when she arrived.`,answer:!1},{text:`All the friendships she made lasted.`,answer:!1},{text:`She accepted invitations even when she was tired.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-107`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says replacement can cost "up to half a person's annual salary", people leave for "three reasons", and some "use exit interviews".`,passage:`Employee turnover is expensive for companies. Recruiting and training a replacement can cost up to half a person's annual salary, and productivity falls while the new employee learns the job. Surveys show that people leave mainly for three reasons: low pay, poor management and lack of opportunities to develop. Companies that listen to staff and offer training generally keep their employees longer. Some use exit interviews to learn why people leave, though critics say that people are rarely honest when they are about to go.`,statements:[{text:`Replacing an employee can be costly.`,answer:!0},{text:`Pay is the only reason people leave jobs.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some companies use exit interviews to learn why people go.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-108`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says urban areas are "several degrees warmer", measures "can reduce temperatures noticeably", but "will not be enough unless emissions also fall".`,passage:`Heatwaves are becoming more frequent and more intense, and cities are particularly affected. Concrete and tarmac absorb heat during the day and release it slowly at night, so urban areas can be several degrees warmer than the surrounding countryside. This "heat island effect" is dangerous for elderly people and those with heart problems. Some cities are planting trees, painting roofs white and building shaded public spaces. Researchers say that these measures can reduce temperatures noticeably, but they warn that they will not be enough unless greenhouse gas emissions also fall.`,statements:[{text:`Cities are cooler than the countryside during heatwaves.`,answer:!1},{text:`White roofs and trees can help to lower temperatures.`,answer:!0},{text:`Researchers think local measures alone will solve the problem.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-109`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "hikers... carry paper maps", pilots "check printed charts", and a paper map gives "a better overview of an area than a small screen".`,passage:`Online maps have made paper maps almost obsolete for ordinary travellers, but professional users still depend on them. Hikers in remote areas carry paper maps because phones can run out of battery or lose their signal. Pilots check printed charts in case of instrument failure. Cartographers say that a paper map also gives a better overview of an area than a small screen: users see the landscape as a whole and can plan a route more easily.`,statements:[{text:`Paper maps are still used by hikers.`,answer:!0},{text:`Pilots never use printed charts.`,answer:!1},{text:`Cartographers believe screens give a better overview.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-110`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the ice is "thinner and less predictable", younger people choose "tourism or the oil industry", and villages have programmes where elders "teach them".`,passage:`In the past, people who lived in the Arctic depended on hunting seals and whales. Today, climate change is making ice thinner and less predictable, which makes traditional hunting dangerous. Younger members of the community are increasingly choosing jobs in tourism or the oil industry. Elders worry that essential skills, such as reading the ice and navigating by the stars, will be lost. Some villages have therefore created programmes in which elders take teenagers on hunting trips and teach them how to survive safely.`,statements:[{text:`Climate change has made hunting on the ice safer.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some young people now work in tourism or the oil industry.`,answer:!0},{text:`Elders refuse to teach their skills to teenagers.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-111`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "more than a hundred small islands", "a system of mobile barriers has been constructed", and it "is expensive to operate".`,passage:`Venice is built on more than a hundred small islands, linked by bridges and canals. The city floods regularly, particularly in autumn, and the problem is getting worse as sea levels rise. A system of mobile barriers has been constructed to protect the lagoon from the highest tides, but it is expensive to operate and its effect on the lagoon's ecosystem is uncertain. Meanwhile, residents have grown used to walking on raised wooden platforms when the water comes up into the streets.`,statements:[{text:`Venice is built on a single large island.`,answer:!1},{text:`A system of barriers has been built to protect the lagoon.`,answer:!0},{text:`The barriers are cheap to operate.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-112`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the chatbot "transfers the conversation to a human", staff "are now dealing with more difficult cases", and unions "fear" the loss of jobs.`,passage:`The company's customer service department has replaced many of its phone operators with a chatbot. The program can answer simple questions about opening hours and deliveries in seconds, and customers do not have to wait in a queue. When a problem is complicated, the chatbot transfers the conversation to a human. The company says that customer satisfaction has remained stable and that the staff who were previously answering routine questions are now dealing with more difficult cases. Trade unions, however, fear that the number of jobs will continue to decrease.`,statements:[{text:`The chatbot deals with all questions without human help.`,answer:!1},{text:`Staff now handle more difficult cases.`,answer:!0},{text:`Trade unions are delighted with the change.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-113`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says talking loudly "is considered rude", newcomers learn "by making a mistake", and the silence is "the only way to share such a small space politely".`,passage:`Rush-hour trains in the capital are so crowded that commuters have developed unwritten rules. People stand facing the doors, keep their bags between their feet and avoid eye contact. Talking loudly on the phone is considered rude, and anyone who plays music without headphones will receive angry looks. Newcomers soon learn the rules, often by making a mistake on their first day. Foreign visitors sometimes find the silence strange, but locals say that it is the only way to share such a small space politely.`,statements:[{text:`Commuters talk loudly to pass the time.`,answer:!1},{text:`Newcomers often learn the rules by making a mistake.`,answer:!0},{text:`Locals think the silence is impolite.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-114`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the best moments... are unplanned", "surprise makes memories stronger", and experts suggest booking "the first and last nights" only.`,passage:`Many travellers report that the best moments of a trip are unplanned. A missed train leads to a conversation with a stranger, a wrong turn reveals a hidden square, a street party appears from nowhere. Psychologists say that surprise makes memories stronger. Even so, most people still plan their journeys carefully, and spend hours reading reviews. Some travel experts recommend a compromise: book the first and last nights, but leave the days between open, so that there is room for discovery without the stress of finding a bed late at night.`,statements:[{text:`The best moments of a trip are usually the planned ones.`,answer:!1},{text:`Surprise can make memories stronger.`,answer:!0},{text:`Travel experts advise booking every night in advance.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-115`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says walkers and cyclists "arrive... in a better mood", critics say it "does not help employees who live far away", and "some even offer small financial rewards".`,passage:`A survey of commuters found that those who walk or cycle to work arrive at the office in a better mood than those who drive or take crowded trains. They also report higher levels of energy in the afternoon. Employers have started to encourage active commuting by installing showers and secure bicycle parking. Some even offer small financial rewards. Critics point out that the approach works well for people who live near their workplace but does not help employees who live far away.`,statements:[{text:`Commuters who walk or cycle are in a better mood.`,answer:!0},{text:`The approach helps all employees equally.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some employers offer rewards for active commuting.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-116`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "more than twenty thousand islands", residents "use boats in the same way that people... use buses", and islanders "always keep a supply of food".`,passage:`There are more than twenty thousand islands off the coast of Norway, and a ferry service links many of them. Travelling between islands is part of everyday life for the residents, who use boats in the same way that people elsewhere use buses. Tourists can join them for a small fee and enjoy a close view of the coastline. Some islands have only a handful of inhabitants, and in winter, storms can cancel ferries for days. For this reason, islanders always keep a supply of food at home.`,statements:[{text:`There are fewer than a thousand islands off Norway.`,answer:!1},{text:`Residents use ferries like buses.`,answer:!0},{text:`Islanders do not need to keep extra food.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-117`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "not salary but the relationship with one's immediate manager", supported employees "were more likely to stay", and companies "train managers in communication skills".`,passage:`A well-known study of workplace happiness found that the most important factor was not salary but the relationship with one's immediate manager. Employees who felt that their manager listened to them and recognised their efforts were far more likely to stay in the job, even when offered a higher salary elsewhere. Managers who were remote or critical had the opposite effect. As a result, many companies have started to train managers in communication skills as well as technical ones.`,statements:[{text:`Salary was found to be the most important factor.`,answer:!1},{text:`Employees with supportive managers were more likely to stay.`,answer:!0},{text:`Companies ignore managers' communication skills.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-118`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "chewing gum is banned", the policy "began in the 1960s", and "residents tend to accept them".`,passage:`The first thing many visitors notice in Singapore is how clean it is. Chewing gum is banned, and heavy fines are imposed on people who drop litter. The city's public transport is fast and reliable, and a large part of the territory is covered with trees, parks and gardens. The policy of making the city green began in the 1960s with a simple campaign to plant trees along roads. Today, buildings with plants on the walls and roofs are common. Some visitors think the rules are strict, but residents tend to accept them.`,statements:[{text:`Chewing gum is permitted in Singapore.`,answer:!1},{text:`The green policy began in the 1960s.`,answer:!0},{text:`Residents generally reject the strict rules.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-119`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says players "scored higher", those who played for more than five hours "slept less", and the authors recommend "limits rather than ban".`,passage:`Video games are often criticised for wasting time, but a recent study of teenagers found some benefits. Players of strategy games scored higher on tests of planning and quick decision-making, while those who played with friends online reported stronger social connections. The researchers are careful to add that these benefits appear only when gaming is moderate. Teenagers who played for more than five hours a day slept less and had lower marks at school. The authors recommend that parents set limits rather than ban games completely, since a total ban often leads to arguments.`,statements:[{text:`The study found no benefits from video games at all.`,answer:!1},{text:`Heavy gaming was linked to less sleep.`,answer:!0},{text:`The authors advise parents to forbid games completely.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-120`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "a good apology has three parts", excuses "weaken the message", and sincere apologisers "often earn more respect".`,passage:`Many people find it difficult to apologise. They worry that saying sorry makes them look weak, or they fear the other person will not accept it. Psychologists say that a good apology has three parts: admitting what you did wrong, showing that you understand the effect on the other person, and offering to make things better. Excuses and phrases like "I'm sorry if you were upset" weaken the message. Research shows that people who apologise sincerely are usually forgiven faster and often earn more respect than before.`,statements:[{text:`A good apology contains three parts.`,answer:!0},{text:`Excuses make an apology more effective.`,answer:!1},{text:`Sincere apologies can increase respect.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-121`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says shoppers "were divided", the best solution is "a bag that is reused many times", and bag use "dropped by eighty per cent".`,passage:`When a large chain of supermarkets announced that it would stop selling single-use plastic bags, shoppers were divided. Environmental groups welcomed the decision, but some customers complained that the new paper bags tear easily and cost more. Researchers pointed out that the best solution is neither plastic nor paper, but a bag that is reused many times. Within six months, the chain reported that the number of bags used had dropped by eighty per cent, mainly because more customers brought their own.`,statements:[{text:`Everyone approved of the supermarket's decision.`,answer:!1},{text:`Researchers believe that reusing bags is the best option.`,answer:!0},{text:`The number of bags used remained about the same.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-122`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says rates "have risen", experts blame "a combination" of causes, and doctors prefer "lasting changes in lifestyle instead of short-term diets".`,passage:`Obesity rates have risen in many countries over the past fifty years. Experts blame a combination of cheaper processed food, bigger portions and less physical activity. Some governments have responded with taxes on sugary drinks and rules on advertising to children. The measures have had limited success so far, partly because the causes are so numerous. Doctors stress that treatment is more effective when it concentrates on lasting changes in lifestyle instead of short-term diets.`,statements:[{text:`Obesity rates have fallen in the past fifty years.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts mention several causes of obesity.`,answer:!0},{text:`Doctors favour short-term diets.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-123`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says small teams "are more innovative", some firms divide staff "into groups of no more than eight", and groups "may duplicate work".`,passage:`Large organisations sometimes find that small teams are more innovative than big departments. In a small team, people know each other well, decisions are made quickly and nobody can hide behind others. Some companies deliberately divide their staff into groups of no more than eight, giving each group a clear goal and freedom to choose its methods. The risk is that groups may duplicate work or pull in different directions, so managers must make sure that goals are linked to the overall strategy.`,statements:[{text:`Small teams can be more innovative.`,answer:!0},{text:`Companies never divide staff into small groups.`,answer:!1},{text:`There is a risk of duplicated work.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-124`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "1970s and 1980s", "some drivers protested", and opposition "falls as people see the benefits".`,passage:`Wearing a seat belt became compulsory in many countries during the 1970s and 1980s. At first, some drivers protested that it limited their freedom. Today the vast majority of people buckle up automatically, and studies show that seat belts have saved hundreds of thousands of lives. Safety experts say that the same pattern is likely with other rules, such as bans on using phones while driving: opposition falls as people see the benefits and as the new behaviour becomes a habit.`,statements:[{text:`Seat belts became compulsory in the 1990s.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some drivers first objected to wearing them.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts expect opposition to similar rules to decrease.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-125`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says vaccination "eliminated smallpox", vaccines "occasionally cause mild reactions", and campaigns "focus on listening to parents' worries".`,passage:`Vaccination programmes have eliminated smallpox and almost ended polio, yet some parents refuse to vaccinate their children because they fear side effects. Doctors acknowledge that vaccines occasionally cause mild reactions, such as a sore arm or a slight fever, but they stress that serious problems are extremely rare compared with the dangers of the diseases themselves. Public health campaigns now focus on listening to parents' worries instead of simply telling them to comply, and early results suggest that this approach is more persuasive.`,statements:[{text:`Smallpox has been eliminated by vaccination.`,answer:!0},{text:`Doctors say that vaccines never cause any reaction.`,answer:!1},{text:`Campaigns now try to listen to parents' worries.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-126`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says early computers "filled entire rooms", early users "wrote their own programs", and some experts "regret" that users do not understand them.`,passage:`The first generation of computers filled entire rooms and were operated by teams of specialists. They were used mainly by governments and large companies, who could afford the enormous cost. It was only in the 1970s that smaller machines appeared, and in the following decade home computers became widely available. Early users often wrote their own programs, since there was little ready-made software. Today most people use computers without understanding how they work, which some experts regret because it makes users dependent on the companies that supply the technology.`,statements:[{text:`The earliest computers were small enough for home use.`,answer:!1},{text:`Early home computer users often wrote their own programs.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts all agree that users should not understand their computers.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-127`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the change "demands more time from managers", "goals can be changed", and staff find the talks "less stressful".`,passage:`A large insurance company has replaced its annual performance review with short monthly conversations between managers and staff. Previously, employees dreaded the yearly meeting, because a single score decided their bonus and could not be discussed afterwards. In the new system, managers give feedback while a project is still running, and goals can be changed if circumstances alter. Staff say the talks are less stressful and more useful. The company admits that the change demands more time from managers, some of whom would rather return to the old method because it involved fewer meetings.`,statements:[{text:`The new system has fewer meetings than the old one.`,answer:!1},{text:`Goals can be adjusted during the year.`,answer:!0},{text:`Employees prefer the old annual review.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-128`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the result "is often the opposite", firms have "a mixed approach", and "the key is variety".`,passage:`In many offices, open-plan layouts were introduced to encourage collaboration. Studies, however, suggest that the result is often the opposite. Workers wear headphones to block out noise, send messages rather than speak, and complain that they cannot concentrate. Some companies have returned to a mixed approach: quiet rooms for focused work, meeting areas for discussion and a few desks that anyone can use. Designers say that the key is variety, because people do different tasks during the day and need different surroundings.`,statements:[{text:`Open-plan offices always increase collaboration.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some firms now offer a mix of quiet and meeting spaces.`,answer:!0},{text:`Designers think a single type of space is ideal.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-129`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "yet studies show that rural areas have their own pressures", and stress comes from "noise, poor housing and little green space".`,passage:`Many people think that city life is always more stressful than country life. Yet studies show that rural areas have their own pressures: isolation, long distances to hospitals and shops, and fewer job opportunities. In cities, on the other hand, access to services is better, and people are surrounded by others. The most stressful places, researchers say, are not specific cities or villages but areas with high levels of noise, poor housing and little green space, wherever they are.`,statements:[{text:`City life is always more stressful than country life.`,answer:!1},{text:`Rural areas have their own pressures.`,answer:!0},{text:`Stress depends partly on noise, housing and green space.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-130`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the job is "to improve staff wellbeing", sceptics say it avoids "low pay and long hours", and successful officers have "real power to change the way work is organised".`,passage:`In recent years, many large companies have appointed a "chief happiness officer" whose job is to improve staff wellbeing. The role may include organising social events, arranging flexible hours and listening to complaints. Supporters say that happy employees are more productive and less likely to leave. Sceptics argue that this is a way of avoiding more serious issues, such as low pay and long hours. The most successful officers, according to one study, are those who have real power to change the way work is organised.`,statements:[{text:`A chief happiness officer is responsible for staff wellbeing.`,answer:!0},{text:`Sceptics believe the role solves low pay and long hours.`,answer:!1},{text:`Officers are most successful when they have real power.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-131`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "more than half" did not know their neighbours, residents eat together "three times a week", and spaces must be "designed carefully".`,passage:`In a recent survey, more than half of adults said they had no idea who lived next door. Sociologists link this isolation to long working hours and the habit of spending free time online rather than in the neighbourhood. One housing project in Copenhagen has tried to reverse the trend. Residents share a large kitchen and eat together three times a week, while their private flats are small. At first, some people found the arrangement strange, but after a year most of them said they felt safer and less lonely. The architects insist that shared spaces must be designed carefully, otherwise nobody uses them.`,statements:[{text:`Most adults in the survey knew their neighbours well.`,answer:!1},{text:`Residents of the Copenhagen project eat together several times a week.`,answer:!0},{text:`Shared spaces always work, however they are designed.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-132`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they "protect villages from storms", land was cleared for "shrimp farms", and "planting is only half the job".`,passage:`Mangrove forests, which grow along tropical coasts, protect villages from storms and provide a nursery for fish. Despite this, around one third of the world's mangroves have disappeared in the last fifty years, mostly because land has been cleared for shrimp farms. In some regions, local communities have started replanting the trees. They have discovered that planting is only half the job: young trees die unless the water flow is restored, so volunteers now spend as much time digging channels as they do planting.`,statements:[{text:`Mangroves protect coastal villages from storms.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most mangrove loss was caused by natural disasters.`,answer:!1},{text:`Planting trees alone is enough to restore a mangrove forest.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-133`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the statistics do not support it", psychologists call it "confirmation bias", and the story is "especially" popular among night-shift workers.`,passage:`Many people believe that the moon influences human behaviour, for example that more babies are born or more crimes committed during a full moon. Hospitals and police forces have examined this idea carefully, but the statistics do not support it. The belief persists because we notice coincidences and forget the many full moons when nothing unusual happened. Psychologists call this "confirmation bias": we remember events that confirm what we already think and ignore the rest. Even so, the story remains popular, especially among night-shift workers who swear that their busiest nights are always full-moon nights.`,statements:[{text:`Statistics confirm that crime rises at full moon.`,answer:!1},{text:`Confirmation bias helps to explain the belief.`,answer:!0},{text:`Night-shift workers are the only people who believe the story.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-134`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says cats "rotate their bodies", "do not always escape injury", and falls from great heights cause "fewer injuries than from medium heights".`,passage:`Rumours say that cats always land on their feet, and scientists have confirmed that they have an impressive ability to turn in mid-air. They use a combination of flexible spines and quick reflexes to rotate their bodies. However, cats do not always escape injury. Vets report that cats falling from great heights suffer fewer injuries than from medium heights, because they have time to relax their bodies. Even so, it would be wrong to assume that a cat can survive any fall.`,statements:[{text:`Cats can turn their bodies in mid-air.`,answer:!0},{text:`Cats are never injured in falls.`,answer:!1},{text:`Falls from medium heights can be more dangerous than from great heights.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-135`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says walkers come "from every continent", paths are "marked with yellow arrows", and the route "takes about a month".`,passage:`The Camino de Santiago attracts walkers from every continent. The most popular route begins in France and takes about a month on foot. Along the way, pilgrims stay in simple hostels, eat in small village restaurants and share stories with strangers. Some walk for religious reasons, but many come to think about a difficult change in their life. The paths are mostly well marked with yellow arrows, though in bad weather the route can become muddy and tiring.`,statements:[{text:`Pilgrims walk the Camino from only one continent.`,answer:!1},{text:`The route is marked with yellow arrows.`,answer:!0},{text:`The most popular route takes about a week.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-136`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says colleagues rated candidates "anonymously", "some quiet employees were rated highly", and critics worry ratings "can be used to settle personal arguments".`,passage:`An engineering firm recently tested a new method for selecting staff for promotion. Instead of relying on managers' opinions, it asked colleagues to rate each candidate anonymously on teamwork, reliability and creativity. The results were sometimes unexpected: some popular managers received low scores, and some quiet employees were rated highly by their peers. The firm says that the method has made the process fairer, although critics worry that anonymous ratings can be used to settle personal arguments.`,statements:[{text:`Managers alone decided on promotions under the new method.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some quiet employees were rated highly by colleagues.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics believe the method cannot be abused.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-137`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says sharks "are harmless to humans", swimmers "must not touch the animals", and biologists asked to "limit the number of daily trips".`,passage:`Whale sharks, the largest fish in the world, visit certain coastal waters every year to feed on tiny organisms. Tourists pay to swim with them, a thrilling experience since the sharks can be twelve metres long, though they are harmless to humans. Guides insist on rules: swimmers must keep a distance of three metres and must not touch the animals. Marine biologists warn that too many boats can disturb the sharks and have asked local authorities to limit the number of daily trips.`,statements:[{text:`Whale sharks are harmless to people.`,answer:!0},{text:`Swimmers are allowed to touch the sharks.`,answer:!1},{text:`Biologists want to limit the number of trips.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-138`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "constant exposure can harm health", cities are trying "quieter road surfaces", and children "have more difficulty concentrating".`,passage:`Noise is often regarded as a minor irritation, but research suggests that constant exposure can harm health. People living near airports or busy roads have higher blood pressure and a greater risk of heart disease. Children in noisy schools tend to have more difficulty concentrating. Cities are therefore trying new solutions, such as quieter road surfaces, sound barriers and limits on night-time flights. Ordinary citizens can also help by using headphones, closing windows at night and avoiding unnecessary use of loud machinery in residential areas.`,statements:[{text:`Noise is a harmless irritation for most people.`,answer:!1},{text:`Quieter road surfaces are among the solutions mentioned.`,answer:!0},{text:`Children in noisy schools concentrate more easily.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-139`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "most of the entries are short: the weather, what he ate", "forty notebooks", and "we plan to turn them into a family book".`,passage:`My grandfather kept a diary every day for sixty years. When he died, we found forty notebooks on a shelf. We expected to read dramatic stories about his life, but most of the entries are short: the weather, what he ate, who visited. Yet reading them has been strangely moving. In these ordinary details, we see the rhythm of his days and the small pleasures that made him happy. My sister has started typing them up, and we plan to turn them into a family book.`,statements:[{text:`The diaries contain mostly dramatic stories.`,answer:!1},{text:`The writer found forty notebooks.`,answer:!0},{text:`The family plans to turn the diaries into a book.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-140`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it "develops gradually", audiologists recommend a test "for anyone over fifty", and aids are "small, comfortable and almost invisible".`,passage:`Hearing loss often goes unnoticed for years because it develops gradually. People may start to turn up the television or ask others to repeat themselves, but many blame these changes on mumbling speakers or noisy restaurants. Audiologists recommend a hearing test for anyone over fifty, even if they do not feel any difficulty, because untreated hearing loss has been linked to loneliness and a higher risk of memory problems. Modern hearing aids are small, comfortable and almost invisible, yet many people delay buying them because they associate them with old age.`,statements:[{text:`Hearing loss usually appears suddenly.`,answer:!1},{text:`Audiologists suggest tests for people over fifty.`,answer:!0},{text:`Modern hearing aids are large and uncomfortable.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-141`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says wind farms generate power "without producing carbon dioxide", "careful planning... reduces the risk to wildlife", and nearby residents "are often more positive".`,passage:`Wind farms generate electricity without producing carbon dioxide, and the number of installations has grown rapidly. Critics complain that turbines spoil the landscape and can kill birds. Developers answer that careful planning, such as choosing sites away from migration routes, reduces the risk to wildlife. In addition, wind farms in the sea cause fewer complaints, because few people live nearby. A recent survey showed that people who live near turbines are often more positive about them than those who have never seen them.`,statements:[{text:`Wind farms produce a lot of carbon dioxide.`,answer:!1},{text:`Careful planning can reduce the risk to birds.`,answer:!0},{text:`People near turbines are always more negative than others.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-142`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says residents "may own a vehicle", "two thirds of households... not to own a car", and "some residents say the system is inconvenient".`,passage:`The village of Vauban in Germany was built on the site of an old military base, with a clear aim: to reduce the use of cars. Residents may own a vehicle, but they must park it in a multi-storey car park at the edge of the village and pay for a space. Streets are used by children, cyclists and trams, and about two thirds of households have decided not to own a car at all. Visitors often describe the atmosphere as calm and friendly. Some residents, however, say that the system is inconvenient when they carry heavy shopping or have elderly relatives.`,statements:[{text:`Residents of Vauban cannot own cars.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most households have chosen to live without a car.`,answer:!0},{text:`All residents think the car-free system is perfect.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-143`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says queues "have become much shorter", images are stored "for several weeks", and some studies suggest the software is "less accurate" for some people.`,passage:`Facial recognition technology is now used at airports to speed up passport control. Travellers look at a camera, and the system compares their face with the photograph stored in their passport. Officials say that queues have become much shorter. However, privacy groups point out that the images are stored for several weeks, and they fear the data could be used for purposes other than border security. Some studies also suggest that the software is less accurate for people with darker skin, which could lead to unfair delays. The authorities promise to review the system every year.`,statements:[{text:`The technology has made queues longer.`,answer:!1},{text:`The images are stored for a period of time.`,answer:!0},{text:`Studies show the software is equally accurate for everyone.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-144`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "I felt a little nervous", "my battery died as I reached the till", and the cashier "offered to charge my phone".`,passage:`The first time I used a smartphone to pay for coffee, I felt a little nervous. I held the phone near the machine, heard a short beep and walked away, half expecting someone to run after me. Within a week it felt completely normal, and I stopped carrying cash. Last month, though, my battery died as I reached the till, and I realised that I had no wallet, no card and no way to pay. The cashier smiled and offered to charge my phone while I waited.`,statements:[{text:`The writer felt confident the first time they used the phone to pay.`,answer:!1},{text:`The writer's phone ran out of battery at the till.`,answer:!0},{text:`The cashier refused to help.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-145`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says passengers "pay extra for luggage, seat selection", some have bags that "must be paid for", and airlines say extras "are optional".`,passage:`Budget airlines have changed the way people travel, but they have also changed the rules. Tickets may cost very little, but passengers pay extra for luggage, seat selection and even a printed boarding pass. Critics say that the final price is often misleading. Passengers who do not read the conditions carefully can arrive at the airport and discover that their cabin bag is too large and must be paid for at the desk. The airlines reply that extras are optional and that customers who travel light still pay very little.`,statements:[{text:`Budget airlines include all extras in the ticket price.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some passengers have to pay at the airport for a large cabin bag.`,answer:!0},{text:`Airlines say that all extras are compulsory.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-146`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says phones "stop charging automatically", "the real enemy of a battery is heat", and deep discharges "put stress on the cells".`,passage:`Many people believe that charging a phone overnight damages the battery, but experts say this is largely a myth. Modern phones stop charging automatically when the battery is full. The real enemy of a battery is heat. Leaving a phone in a hot car or under a pillow while it charges can shorten its life far more than any overnight habit. Manufacturers also advise against letting the battery fall to zero too often, since deep discharges put stress on the cells. Keeping the charge between twenty and eighty per cent is considered ideal.`,statements:[{text:`Phones keep charging even when the battery is full.`,answer:!1},{text:`Heat is a bigger threat to batteries than overnight charging.`,answer:!0},{text:`Letting the battery empty often is recommended.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-147`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says children "often overestimate their ability", parents "can turn the journey into a lesson", and experts "recommend... with an adult".`,passage:`Crossing the road is something we do without thinking, but for young children it is a complex skill. They must judge the speed of cars, decide how much time they have and control their impulse to run. Research shows that children under ten often overestimate their ability, which is why road safety experts recommend that they should always cross with an adult. Parents who walk with their children can turn the journey into a lesson by asking them to say when they think it is safe and then explaining the decision.`,statements:[{text:`Children under ten always judge traffic well.`,answer:!1},{text:`Parents can use walks to teach road safety.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts say children should cross alone from an early age.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-148`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says guests leave phones "in a locker", waiters are "blind or partially sighted", and "some people find the experience uncomfortable".`,passage:`A new restaurant in the city serves its meals in total darkness. Guests leave their phones and bags in a locker, and blind or partially sighted waiters guide them to their tables. Without sight, diners say that tastes and smells become more intense, and conversation is livelier because there are no distractions. Some people find the experience uncomfortable at first, and a few leave early. However, most describe it as memorable and say that it changed the way they think about disability.`,statements:[{text:`Guests can use their phones at the table.`,answer:!1},{text:`The waiters are blind or partially sighted.`,answer:!0},{text:`Everyone enjoys the experience from the beginning.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-149`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says some "choose it freely", that "the main obstacle is money", and that employers "are slowly learning".`,passage:`The number of people who change careers in their forties has risen steadily. Some are forced to do so when industries decline, but many choose it freely because they want more meaning in their work. Career advisers say that mature workers have a clear advantage: they are reliable and already know how to deal with difficult colleagues. The main obstacle is money. Retraining can be expensive, and a lower starting salary may be hard to accept after years of seniority. Employers, meanwhile, are slowly learning that a candidate with an unusual background can bring fresh ideas to a team.`,statements:[{text:`All career changes in middle age are forced on workers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Money is described as the main obstacle to retraining.`,answer:!0},{text:`Employers have always welcomed candidates with unusual backgrounds.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-150`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "with no reduction in pay", "a drop in staff turnover", and critics say it would be "impossible in hospitals or factories".`,passage:`A small software company recently introduced a four-day working week with no reduction in pay. Managers expected productivity to fall, but after six months the company reported higher profits, fewer sick days and a drop in staff turnover. Employees said they were more focused during the working days because they knew they had a long weekend ahead. Critics argue that the system works only in offices and would be impossible in hospitals or factories, where work has to be done every day.`,statements:[{text:`The company reduced salaries when it introduced the four-day week.`,answer:!1},{text:`Fewer employees left the company after the change.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics think the system could easily be used in hospitals.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-151`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says slow travel means "staying longer in fewer places", it "reduces the number of flights and train journeys", and travellers come home "more relaxed".`,passage:`Many tourists make the mistake of trying to see everything in a few days. They rush from monument to monument, take photographs and move on without really experiencing the place. Travel writers recommend "slow travel": staying longer in fewer places, walking, talking to locals and trying small neighbourhood restaurants. This approach is also more sustainable, as it reduces the number of flights and train journeys. Those who have tried it say they come home more relaxed and with richer memories than after a rushed itinerary.`,statements:[{text:`Slow travel involves visiting as many places as possible.`,answer:!1},{text:`Slow travel can reduce the number of journeys.`,answer:!0},{text:`Slow travellers come home more tired.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-152`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says emissions come "mainly because of air travel", critics argue offsetting gives "a clear conscience", and groups suggest to "travel less".`,passage:`Tourism accounts for roughly eight per cent of global greenhouse gas emissions, mainly because of air travel. Some travel companies now offer carbon offsetting, which allows customers to pay a small fee that funds tree planting or renewable energy projects. Supporters say it is a practical way to take responsibility. Critics argue that it gives passengers a clear conscience without reducing flights, and that some projects do not deliver the promised benefits. Environmental groups suggest that the best option is to travel less, choose trains when possible and stay longer in each destination.`,statements:[{text:`Most tourism emissions come from hotels.`,answer:!1},{text:`Carbon offsetting is criticised by some people.`,answer:!0},{text:`Environmental groups recommend taking more short flights.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-153`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the time was cut "from an hour to ten minutes", "the old ferry... has stopped running", and "one of the former ferrymen now works there as a guide".`,passage:`A new bridge across the river has cut the journey time between two towns from an hour to ten minutes. Local businesses welcomed the change because customers can now reach them more easily. However, the old ferry, which had carried passengers for more than a century, has stopped running, and the ferrymen have lost their jobs. A small museum has been created to preserve the history of the crossing, and one of the former ferrymen now works there as a guide, telling visitors what the river looked like before the bridge.`,statements:[{text:`The bridge has made the journey longer.`,answer:!1},{text:`The ferry service has ended.`,answer:!0},{text:`None of the ferrymen found new work.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-154`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "I used to hate going to the dentist", "I have never used it" (the signal), and music was helpful during treatment.`,passage:`I used to hate going to the dentist, but a change in my attitude helped. Instead of imagining the worst, I started to listen to music through headphones during treatment, and I asked the dentist to explain what he was doing. The sense of control reduced my anxiety enormously. He also suggested a signal I could make with my hand if I needed a pause. I have never used it, but knowing that it exists makes the whole visit calmer. I now go for my check-ups every six months without worrying.`,statements:[{text:`The writer enjoyed going to the dentist from the start.`,answer:!1},{text:`The writer uses a hand signal during treatment.`,answer:!1},{text:`Listening to music helped the writer to relax.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-155`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says staff "may take home any unsold bread", extra "is given to a local shelter", and "costs are almost unchanged".`,passage:`A bakery in Norway has introduced a rule: employees may take home any unsold bread at the end of the day. Before the policy, tonnes of loaves were thrown away every week. Now the staff take bread for their families, and any extra is given to a local shelter. The owner says that the policy has increased loyalty among workers and improved the bakery's reputation. Costs are almost unchanged, since the bread would otherwise have been wasted.`,statements:[{text:`Employees must pay for unsold bread.`,answer:!1},{text:`Extra bread goes to a local shelter.`,answer:!0},{text:`The policy has increased costs significantly.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-156`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says bags "take hundreds of years to break down", usage "has fallen sharply" after charges, and environmentalists want "a wider effort to reduce all single-use plastics".`,passage:`Plastic bags take hundreds of years to break down, and millions end up in the oceans every year. Turtles mistake them for jellyfish and eat them. Since several countries introduced a small charge for bags, usage has fallen sharply: in one nation, by more than eighty per cent in the first year. Environmentalists welcome the change but say that it should be part of a wider effort to reduce all single-use plastics, including packaging and bottles.`,statements:[{text:`Plastic bags break down quickly.`,answer:!1},{text:`Charges for bags have reduced their use.`,answer:!0},{text:`Environmentalists think bags are the only problem.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-157`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says trains "produce far fewer emissions", tickets "can be much more expensive", and campaigners urge governments "to lower rail fares or to tax aviation fuel".`,passage:`High-speed trains now connect many European cities in a few hours. For distances of up to about eight hundred kilometres, they can be as fast as flying once the time spent at airports is included, and they produce far fewer emissions. Passengers also appreciate the comfort and the opportunity to work. The main drawback is the price: tickets for popular routes can be much more expensive than low-cost flights. Campaigners urge governments to lower rail fares or to tax aviation fuel, so that the environmentally friendly option becomes the cheaper one.`,statements:[{text:`Trains produce more emissions than planes on short routes.`,answer:!1},{text:`Rail tickets can be more expensive than low-cost flights.`,answer:!0},{text:`Campaigners want governments to make flying cheaper.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-158`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the coating "lowers the surface temperature", measures "use no energy", and some residents "complain that the white paint is too bright".`,passage:`Cities in hot climates are experimenting with new ways to keep their residents cool. In one city, streets have been painted with a light-coloured reflective coating that lowers the surface temperature by several degrees. In another, giant shade structures have been placed over bus stops and playgrounds. Planners say that these measures are cheaper than air conditioning and use no energy. Residents, however, are divided: some praise the improvements, while others complain that the white paint is too bright and hurts their eyes.`,statements:[{text:`The reflective paint raises the temperature of the street.`,answer:!1},{text:`Planners say the measures need no energy.`,answer:!0},{text:`All residents approve of the white paint.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-159`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they moved "to a flat after living in a house", "my father has taken up beekeeping", and they "have more friends in the building".`,passage:`Moving to a flat after living in a house with a garden was a big change for my parents. They missed the space and the flowers, and for the first year they complained about the noise from the street. Then they discovered the community garden behind the building, where residents grow vegetables and herbs. My mother now spends every morning there, and my father has taken up beekeeping on the roof. They say they have more friends in the building than they ever had on their old street.`,statements:[{text:`The parents moved from a flat to a house.`,answer:!1},{text:`The father started beekeeping on the roof.`,answer:!0},{text:`The parents regret moving.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-160`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "comprehension is similar for both", "younger readers in particular are choosing audio", and "a poor voice can spoil a good book".`,passage:`Audiobooks have become a significant part of the publishing industry. Listeners say that they can enjoy stories while commuting, cooking or exercising. Some people worry that listening is not "real" reading, but researchers have found that comprehension is similar for both. A professional narrator can add emotion and character, though a poor voice can spoil a good book. Publishers report that younger readers in particular are choosing audio, and many now release a recording alongside the printed edition.`,statements:[{text:`Researchers found that comprehension is similar for audiobooks and print.`,answer:!0},{text:`Audiobooks are mainly popular among the old.`,answer:!1},{text:`A poor narrator can spoil a book.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-161`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people "woke after a few hours", diaries "mention this first sleep and second sleep", and some experts think waking "may be natural rather than a sign of illness".`,passage:`Sleep scientists say that the habit of sleeping in two separate periods during the night was once normal. Before electric light, people often went to bed soon after dark, woke after a few hours and stayed awake for an hour or two before sleeping again. Historical diaries mention this "first sleep" and "second sleep". The modern habit of sleeping eight hours in one block is relatively recent, and some experts believe that waking in the middle of the night may be natural rather than a sign of illness.`,statements:[{text:`People in the past always slept for eight hours without waking.`,answer:!1},{text:`Historical diaries mention two periods of sleep.`,answer:!0},{text:`All experts agree that night waking is a disease.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-162`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says devices are "difficult to open", laws require parts "for at least seven years", and manufacturers "say that unrestricted repairs may be dangerous".`,passage:`The idea of a "right to repair" is gaining support. At the moment, many manufacturers make their devices difficult to open and refuse to sell spare parts, so customers have to buy a new product when a small part fails. Campaigners argue that this creates unnecessary waste and costs. Several governments have introduced laws that require producers to supply parts and instructions for at least seven years. Manufacturers say that unrestricted repairs may be dangerous and could damage their products' reputation.`,statements:[{text:`Manufacturers usually make repairs easy.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some laws require parts to be supplied for years.`,answer:!0},{text:`Manufacturers fully support the right to repair.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-163`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the journey "takes almost three hours", "the journey is the main attraction", and the company "has no plans to modernise the line".`,passage:`The railway line to the mountain village is only forty kilometres long, but the journey takes almost three hours. The train climbs slowly through tunnels and across bridges that were built more than a century ago. Passengers can see waterfalls and pine forests from the window, and most agree that the journey is the main attraction. At the village, there is little to do except walk and relax, but the quiet is exactly what visitors want. The railway company has no plans to modernise the line, because speeding it up would destroy its charm.`,statements:[{text:`The journey to the village is very short.`,answer:!1},{text:`The scenery is a major attraction of the trip.`,answer:!0},{text:`The company wants to make the train faster.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-164`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she "had never been very sporty", she "joined a running group", and she finished "in just over five hours".`,passage:`Anna had never been very sporty, but when she turned forty she decided to run a marathon. At first she could not run for more than five minutes, and her neighbours looked at her with amusement. She followed a training plan, joined a running group and, after eight months, completed the race in just over five hours. She says the achievement was less about speed than about proving to herself that she could finish. Now she runs three times a week and encourages others who think they are too old or unfit.`,statements:[{text:`Anna was a keen athlete in her youth.`,answer:!1},{text:`Anna joined a running group.`,answer:!0},{text:`She finished the marathon in under four hours.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-165`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says there is "no guaranteed income", experts advise "to save at least six months' salary first", and governments are "offering tax benefits".`,passage:`Self-employed workers now make up a growing proportion of the workforce in many countries. They enjoy independence and flexible hours, but they also face uncertainty: there is no guaranteed income, no paid holiday and no company pension. Some governments are trying to make the system fairer by offering tax benefits and savings plans for the self-employed. Experts advise anyone who is thinking of working for themselves to save at least six months' salary first, to cover difficult periods.`,statements:[{text:`Self-employed workers have guaranteed incomes.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts advise saving before becoming self-employed.`,answer:!0},{text:`Governments are ignoring self-employed workers.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-166`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says allergies "are becoming more common", farm children "are much less likely to suffer from asthma", and doctors "warn parents against abandoning basic cleanliness".`,passage:`Allergies are becoming more common in rich countries, and scientists have several theories. One idea, known as the hygiene hypothesis, suggests that children who grow up in very clean homes have immune systems that are less well trained, so they react to harmless substances such as pollen. Studies of children raised on farms support this view: they are much less likely to suffer from asthma. Doctors, however, warn parents against abandoning basic cleanliness. The hypothesis, they say, is about exposure to a variety of microbes, not about living in dirt.`,statements:[{text:`Allergies are less common in rich countries than before.`,answer:!1},{text:`Children raised on farms are less likely to have asthma.`,answer:!0},{text:`Doctors encourage parents to stop cleaning their homes.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-167`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says there is "a shortage of engineers", the firm "recruits former musicians", and some engineers "were sceptical at first".`,passage:`A shortage of engineers has made some companies look for talent in unexpected places. One firm now recruits former musicians, reasoning that they are used to practising patiently, working in teams and performing under pressure. The company provides several months of training in technical skills, and many musicians have adapted quickly. Managers say that the new employees are creative and persistent. Some traditional engineers were sceptical at first, but they have come to value the different viewpoint that the musicians bring.`,statements:[{text:`The company has too many engineers.`,answer:!1},{text:`The firm recruits former musicians.`,answer:!0},{text:`Traditional engineers have always approved of the plan.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-168`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says traditions "vary widely", in Denmark "a flag is often flown", and customs include being carried, ear pulling and hitting a container.`,passage:`Birthday traditions vary widely around the world. In some countries, the birthday person is lifted up and carried around by friends, while in others the child is expected to pull their own ear a number of times equal to their age. In Denmark, a flag is often flown outside the house, and in Mexico, children enjoy hitting a decorated container filled with sweets. Whatever the custom, the shared idea is that the day should be special and that friends and family should gather.`,statements:[{text:`Birthday customs are the same in most countries.`,answer:!1},{text:`In Denmark, a flag may be flown on a birthday.`,answer:!0},{text:`All birthday customs involve food.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-169`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the method is "far safer", that some species "are frightened by the noise", and that drones are flown "very high".`,passage:`Wildlife biologists have started using drones to count seabirds on remote cliffs. In the past, researchers had to climb dangerous rocks, which was slow and often disturbed the nests. A drone can photograph an entire colony in a few minutes, and software then counts the birds automatically. The method is far safer, but it is not perfect. Some species are frightened by the noise and fly away, which can leave eggs exposed to predators. For this reason, scientists now fly the drones very high, and they avoid the nesting season for the most sensitive birds.`,statements:[{text:`Counting birds by drone is slower than climbing the cliffs.`,answer:!1},{text:`Drones can sometimes frighten the birds.`,answer:!0},{text:`Researchers fly drones low to see the nests clearly.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-170`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the experience "is not cheap", "heavy rain can turn a field into a sea of mud", and organisers use "straw and wooden tracks".`,passage:`Every summer, thousands of people attend open-air music festivals. The experience is not cheap: tickets, transport and food can cost several hundred pounds. Even so, many festival-goers say that the atmosphere and the chance to hear dozens of bands in one weekend make it worthwhile. The weather is a gamble, and heavy rain can turn a field into a sea of mud. Organisers now advise visitors to bring waterproof boots and a spare pair of socks, and they have started using straw and wooden tracks to keep the paths usable.`,statements:[{text:`Festival tickets are usually very cheap.`,answer:!1},{text:`Rain can make a festival site muddy.`,answer:!0},{text:`Organisers do nothing to deal with mud.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-171`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says touring is "away from... expensive lift passes", off-piste skiers "must carry a transceiver, a shovel and a probe", and beginners should "hire a qualified guide".`,passage:`Ski touring, in which skiers climb a mountain with special skins on their skis and then ski down, is becoming more popular. Supporters like the silence and the sense of adventure, away from crowded slopes and expensive lift passes. The activity is not without danger. Avalanches kill several people every winter, and experts insist that anyone who goes off the prepared tracks must carry a transceiver, a shovel and a probe, and know how to use them. Beginners are strongly advised to hire a qualified guide for their first few trips.`,statements:[{text:`Ski touring requires lift passes.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts say that people need safety equipment off the prepared tracks.`,answer:!0},{text:`Beginners are advised to go alone.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-172`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says huts provide "beds and meals", "thunderstorms in the afternoon are common", and guides advise "starting at sunrise".`,passage:`A walking holiday in the Dolomites can be as demanding or as easy as you wish. Mountain huts provide beds and meals, so hikers do not need to carry tents or cooking equipment. Many routes are marked with numbers and colours, and cable cars allow tired walkers to avoid the steepest climbs. The weather can change rapidly, however, and thunderstorms in the afternoon are common. Guides advise starting at sunrise, so that the day's walk is finished before the clouds build up.`,statements:[{text:`Hikers in the Dolomites must carry tents.`,answer:!1},{text:`Afternoon thunderstorms are common.`,answer:!0},{text:`Guides recommend starting late in the day.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-173`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "two people divide the responsibilities of one full-time job", employers "report that it has advantages", and "the difficulties come from communication".`,passage:`Job-sharing is an arrangement in which two people divide the responsibilities of one full-time job. It is popular among parents and older workers who want to reduce their hours without losing their careers. Employers who have tried it report that it has advantages: when one partner is ill or on holiday, the other can take over, and the two often bring different skills. The difficulties come from communication. Partners must hand over their work carefully, and some managers complain that the arrangement means more meetings and emails.`,statements:[{text:`Two people share one job in a job-share.`,answer:!0},{text:`Employers see no benefits in the arrangement.`,answer:!1},{text:`Communication between partners can be a problem.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-174`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "I have never been good", the friend "laughed", and "I have put all important dates in my phone".`,passage:`I have never been good at remembering birthdays, which has caused some awkward moments. Last year, I forgot my best friend's birthday completely and only noticed when I saw photos of her party on social media. I was so embarrassed that I phoned her immediately and apologised. Fortunately, she laughed and said she had expected it. Since then, I have put all important dates in my phone, with a reminder a week in advance, and I have not missed one since.`,statements:[{text:`The writer is usually good at remembering birthdays.`,answer:!1},{text:`The friend was angry about the forgotten birthday.`,answer:!1},{text:`The writer now uses reminders on a phone.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-175`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says hostels are "no longer just for young backpackers", many offer "private rooms with their own bathrooms", and prices "are much lower than in hotels".`,passage:`Hostels are no longer just for young backpackers. Many now offer private rooms with their own bathrooms, along with shared kitchens and common areas, so that older travellers and families can also use them. The social atmosphere remains a big attraction: guests meet in the evening, share advice on what to see and sometimes travel together for a few days. Prices are much lower than in hotels, which allows people to spend their money on experiences. Some travellers say that the only disadvantage is the lack of quiet.`,statements:[{text:`Hostels are only for young backpackers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many hostels offer private rooms.`,answer:!0},{text:`Hostel prices are higher than hotel prices.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-176`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says we use "virtually all of our brain", damage to a small region "can have serious consequences", and learning is "a matter of practice".`,passage:`The idea that people use only ten per cent of their brains is a popular myth. Brain scans show that we use virtually all of our brain, even when we are asleep, although not every area is active at the same time. Damage to even a small region can have serious consequences, which would not be the case if ninety per cent were unused. The myth probably survives because it is encouraging: it suggests that we all have huge, untapped potential. Neuroscientists, however, say that learning is a matter of practice, not of unlocking hidden capacity.`,statements:[{text:`Scans show that people use only ten per cent of their brain.`,answer:!1},{text:`Damage to a small part of the brain can be serious.`,answer:!0},{text:`Neuroscientists believe that hidden capacity can easily be unlocked.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-177`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "often said" (not always), vets advise "share the care among all family members", and "young children cannot manage it alone".`,passage:`Children who grow up with pets are often said to develop more empathy. Studies of schoolchildren suggest that those who care for an animal are better at recognising the feelings of others and are less likely to be bullied. Parents should be careful, however, about expecting too much. A pet is a big responsibility, and young children cannot manage it alone. Vets advise families to choose an animal that suits their lifestyle and to share the care among all family members.`,statements:[{text:`Pets always make children more empathetic.`,answer:!1},{text:`Vets advise sharing the care of a pet.`,answer:!0},{text:`Young children can usually look after a pet alone.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-178`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "about a quarter of the food they buy", "best before... indicates quality, while 'use by'... safety", and families have a "leftover night".`,passage:`Many people feel guilty when they throw away food. A survey in one country found that households waste about a quarter of the food they buy, mostly fresh vegetables, bread and leftovers. Experts say that simple habits can reduce this: making a shopping list, freezing portions, and learning the difference between "best before" and "use by" dates. The first indicates quality, while the second concerns safety. Some families now plan a weekly "leftover night" in which they make a meal from whatever remains in the fridge.`,statements:[{text:`Households waste about a quarter of their food.`,answer:!0},{text:`"Best before" refers to safety.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some families plan a night to use leftovers.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-179`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "around two thirds" in Germany, "in other countries" the figure is low (not everywhere), and experts recommend "convenient systems and clear information instead of lectures".`,passage:`Recycling rates differ widely between countries. In Germany, around two thirds of household waste is recycled, thanks to a deposit system for bottles and clear rules for sorting rubbish. In other countries the figure is below ten per cent, mainly because collection systems are poor. Experts say that the difference is not due to attitudes alone: where recycling is easy and rewarded, people take part. They recommend that governments focus on convenient systems and clear information instead of lectures.`,statements:[{text:`German households recycle around two thirds of their waste.`,answer:!0},{text:`The recycling rate is below ten per cent everywhere else.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts suggest that convenient systems matter more than lectures.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-180`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says silent studiers "remembered about twenty per cent more", music "competes with the brain's language processing", and it "may help with repetitive tasks".`,passage:`A team of researchers asked teenagers to learn a list of words while either listening to music or sitting in silence. The results were clear: those who studied in silence remembered about twenty per cent more words. The researchers explained that music, especially with lyrics, competes with the brain's language processing. However, they added that some teenagers said they felt more motivated with music, and that this may help with repetitive tasks such as cleaning. For serious studying, they recommend a quiet place.`,statements:[{text:`Teenagers learned more words when they listened to music.`,answer:!1},{text:`Music with lyrics can interfere with language processing.`,answer:!0},{text:`The researchers say music is useless in every situation.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-181`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says doctors refer patients "to activities such as gardening clubs", participants "visit their doctors less often", and some doctors "remain unconvinced".`,passage:`The idea of "social prescribing" is gaining ground in health services. Instead of simply giving medicine, doctors refer patients to activities such as gardening clubs, choirs or walking groups. The aim is to reduce loneliness and improve wellbeing, which often have a greater effect on health than people expect. Early studies show that patients who take part visit their doctors less often. Some physicians, however, remain unconvinced, arguing that more evidence is needed before public money is spent on activities that are difficult to measure.`,statements:[{text:`Social prescribing means giving patients extra medicine.`,answer:!1},{text:`Patients who take part may visit doctors less.`,answer:!0},{text:`All physicians are fully convinced of its value.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-182`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "instead of asking about qualifications", candidates worked "with a group of strangers", and employees "have stayed longer and perform better".`,passage:`The company's new recruitment strategy was an unusual one. Instead of asking candidates about their qualifications, the interviewers gave them a practical problem and asked them to work on it for an hour with a group of strangers. They observed who listened to others, who shared ideas and who tried to dominate. Candidates were surprised, and some complained that the task was artificial. The company, however, claims that its employees recruited this way have stayed longer and perform better than those hired through traditional interviews.`,statements:[{text:`Interviewers asked mainly about qualifications.`,answer:!1},{text:`Candidates worked together on a practical problem.`,answer:!0},{text:`The company thinks the new method gives poor results.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-183`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says buses take "longer", coaches "stop in small towns", and "the main drawback is the time".`,passage:`Travelling by bus is cheap, but it takes longer than by train or plane. A long-distance coach from Madrid to Lisbon takes about nine hours and costs a fraction of the price of a flight. Passengers say that the journey is comfortable, with wi-fi, reclining seats and sockets for charging phones. Because the coaches stop in small towns, they also offer an opportunity to see places that rail travellers miss. The main drawback is the time, which makes the bus unsuitable for tight schedules.`,statements:[{text:`Buses are faster than trains and planes.`,answer:!1},{text:`Coaches make stops in small towns.`,answer:!0},{text:`The main drawback of coach travel is the time.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-184`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says roads "can be dangerous", authorities "publish daily road reports", and "some roads in the interior are closed for most of the year".`,passage:`Iceland's roads can be dangerous in winter, and tourists who rent a car need to be well prepared. The weather changes fast, and a clear morning may become a snowstorm by afternoon. Local authorities publish daily road reports, and they advise visitors to check them before setting out. Some roads in the interior are closed for most of the year. Experienced drivers say that the best advice is simple: if you are unsure, wait, and never drive in poor visibility.`,statements:[{text:`Iceland's roads are safe in winter for everyone.`,answer:!1},{text:`The authorities publish daily road reports.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some interior roads are closed for much of the year.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-185`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says preparation "varies enormously", Moroccan tea has "a lot of sugar and [is] poured from a height", and Tibetan tea is mixed "with butter and salt".`,passage:`Tea is the most popular drink in many countries, but the way it is prepared varies enormously. In Britain, it is usually served with milk, and arguments about whether the milk should go in first are traditional. In Morocco, mint tea is made with a lot of sugar and poured from a height to create bubbles. In Tibet, tea is mixed with butter and salt to give energy at high altitude. Visitors are often surprised, but most agree that trying local versions of tea is an easy way to understand a culture.`,statements:[{text:`Tea is prepared in the same way everywhere.`,answer:!1},{text:`In Morocco, tea is sweetened and served from a height.`,answer:!0},{text:`In Tibet, tea is mixed with sugar and milk.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-186`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says that attendance "improved", that the council "offers supervised activities", and that critics think the experiment "cannot be copied in larger cities".`,passage:`When a small town in northern Sweden introduced a four-day school week, many parents feared that pupils would fall behind. After two years, however, the results surprised everyone. Attendance improved, and teachers reported that children were calmer on the days they were in class. The extra day off was not wasted: many pupils joined local sports clubs or helped in family businesses. The council admits that working parents found the change difficult at first, so it now offers supervised activities at the library. Critics argue that the experiment cannot be copied in larger cities, where families have fewer relatives nearby.`,statements:[{text:`Pupils' exam results fell sharply after the change.`,answer:!1},{text:`The council organises activities for children on the extra day off.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics believe the idea would work equally well in big cities.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-187`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the app "works without an internet connection", it "struggles with handwriting", and the version for poor eyesight is being "tested".`,passage:`A small start-up has developed a mobile app that translates street signs in real time. The user simply points the phone camera at a sign, and the translation appears on the screen in the same style of letters. The app works without an internet connection, which makes it attractive to travellers abroad. The developers admit that it struggles with handwriting and with signs written in decorative fonts. They plan to add more languages next year and are also testing a version for people with poor eyesight, which would read the signs aloud.`,statements:[{text:`The app requires a constant internet connection.`,answer:!1},{text:`It has difficulty with handwritten signs.`,answer:!0},{text:`A version that reads aloud is already on sale.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-188`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says residents can reach "work, shops, schools and parks within a quarter of an hour", planners say "nobody will be forced", and Paris shows "a rise in local business".`,passage:`Cities around the world are experimenting with a "15-minute" model, in which residents can reach work, shops, schools and parks within a quarter of an hour on foot or by bicycle. Supporters say that this reduces traffic and strengthens local communities. Opponents fear that it will restrict freedom of movement and create divisions between rich and poor areas. Planners insist that nobody will be forced to stay in their neighbourhood; the aim is simply to provide more choices close to home. Early results from Paris show more people cycling and a rise in local business.`,statements:[{text:`In the 15-minute model, daily needs are close to home.`,answer:!0},{text:`The model forbids people from leaving their neighbourhood.`,answer:!1},{text:`Paris has seen a rise in local business.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-189`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the secret of good cooking is preparation", most of us "start cooking while we are still looking for the garlic", and he suggests "cleaning as you go".`,passage:`My cousin is a professional chef, and he claims that the secret of good cooking is preparation. Before service begins, every ingredient is washed, chopped and measured, a process called "mise en place". At home, most of us start cooking while we are still looking for the garlic, which makes the process stressful. He recommends reading the whole recipe first, putting everything on the table and cleaning as you go. It may seem slow, he says, but it saves time in the end and makes cooking far more enjoyable.`,statements:[{text:`The chef thinks that preparation is the key to good cooking.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most home cooks prepare everything before starting.`,answer:!1},{text:`The chef advises cleaning at the end of the meal.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-190`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "I used to think... lonely", the writer goes "to a small place near my flat", and the regulars just "nod to each other".`,passage:`I used to think that people who sit alone in cafés were lonely. Then I tried it. On Sunday mornings I take a notebook to a small place near my flat, order a coffee and write for an hour. Nobody expects anything from me, and ideas come more easily than at home, where I am distracted by chores. Now I find it hard to understand why I ever felt sorry for those solitary customers. A few of them have become familiar faces, and we nod to each other as we pass, which is enough conversation for me.`,statements:[{text:`The writer once felt sorry for people alone in cafés.`,answer:!0},{text:`The writer writes at home on Sunday mornings.`,answer:!1},{text:`The writer has long conversations with the other regular customers.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-191`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it is "the largest coral system", "rising sea temperatures have caused repeated bleaching", and "only reducing greenhouse gases will protect the reef".`,passage:`The Great Barrier Reef, off the coast of Australia, is the largest coral system in the world. It attracts millions of visitors who dive and snorkel above its colourful corals. However, rising sea temperatures have caused repeated bleaching, in which corals lose their colour and may die. Scientists are experimenting with ways to help the reef, from growing heat-resistant coral in laboratories to shading small areas with a thin film of tiny bubbles. They say these efforts can buy time, but only reducing greenhouse gases will protect the reef in the long term.`,statements:[{text:`The Great Barrier Reef is the smallest coral system.`,answer:!1},{text:`Rising sea temperatures cause coral bleaching.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientists believe laboratory methods alone can save the reef.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-192`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people over sixty-five "give more hours", charities "are finding it harder to recruit younger volunteers", and some "offer short, flexible tasks that can be done online".`,passage:`A recent report on volunteering shows that people over sixty-five give more hours to charity than any other age group. Retired people, the report says, have time and experience to share, and many say that volunteering gives them a sense of purpose after they stop working. However, charities are finding it harder to recruit younger volunteers, who often work long hours and move frequently. To attract them, some organisations now offer short, flexible tasks that can be done online in an evening rather than requiring a commitment for months.`,statements:[{text:`Older people volunteer less than the young.`,answer:!1},{text:`Charities find it easy to recruit young volunteers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some organisations offer short online tasks.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-193`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the ban covered "plastic straws from all its cafés and restaurants", customers "complained", and groups say straws are "only a tiny part of the plastic problem".`,passage:`In 2019, a small town in the north of England banned plastic straws from all its cafés and restaurants. At first, customers complained that paper straws became soggy, but business owners soon found suppliers of better-quality products. The town has since been joined by dozens of others, and the council claims that tens of thousands of plastic items have been prevented from entering the sea. Environmental groups welcome the move, but warn that straws are only a tiny part of the plastic problem.`,statements:[{text:`The ban applied to plastic straws in cafés and restaurants.`,answer:!0},{text:`Customers were happy with paper straws from the start.`,answer:!1},{text:`Environmental groups think straws are the main plastic problem.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-194`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says friends "watched television while eating", the writer will "keep the rule", and at the time the writer "found it unfair".`,passage:`When I was a child, my family had a rule: no screens at meals. At the time I found it unfair, because my friends watched television while eating. Now I understand its value. Dinner was the only time of day when everyone sat down together, and we talked about our days, argued about politics and laughed at old stories. When I have children of my own, I intend to keep the rule, even though I know they will probably complain as I did.`,statements:[{text:`The writer's friends were not allowed screens at meals.`,answer:!1},{text:`The writer plans to introduce the same rule for their own children.`,answer:!0},{text:`The writer always agreed with the rule.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-195`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says he "did not work alone", the team "tested thousands of materials", and "other scientists had produced earlier versions".`,passage:`The engineer who invented the first practical lightbulb did not work alone. Thomas Edison led a team of researchers who tested thousands of materials before they found a filament that lasted. Historians say that Edison's real talent was organising people and turning ideas into products. He also understood publicity: his demonstrations drew crowds and investors. Although he is often credited as the sole inventor, other scientists had produced earlier versions of electric lamps.`,statements:[{text:`Edison worked entirely on his own.`,answer:!1},{text:`Edison tested thousands of materials with his team.`,answer:!0},{text:`Earlier versions of electric lamps already existed.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-196`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the opposite happened", noise "fell by almost half", and the council introduced "a free electric minibus".`,passage:`Last year, a coastal town in Portugal decided to ban cars from its historic centre between June and September. Shopkeepers were afraid that sales would collapse, but the opposite happened. Pedestrians spent more time in the narrow streets, and cafés put tables outside. Noise levels fell by almost half, and residents reported that they slept better. Not everyone is happy: elderly people with limited mobility say it is now harder to reach their doctors, so the council has introduced a free electric minibus that circles the centre every ten minutes.`,statements:[{text:`Shopkeepers' sales fell sharply after the ban.`,answer:!1},{text:`Noise levels dropped noticeably.`,answer:!0},{text:`The council did nothing to help elderly residents.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-197`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says wetlands "filter water, absorb floods", almost half "have been drained", and "farmers were paid to leave part of their land flooded".`,passage:`Wetlands may look like unproductive land, but they provide a range of services. They filter water, absorb floods, store carbon and shelter many species of birds and fish. Over the last century, however, almost half of the world's wetlands have been drained for farming or building. Conservation groups are working with local communities to restore some of them. In one project, farmers were paid to leave part of their land flooded in winter, which brought back migrating birds within two years.`,statements:[{text:`Wetlands provide useful services.`,answer:!0},{text:`Wetlands have increased in the last century.`,answer:!1},{text:`Farmers were paid to leave some land flooded.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-198`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it is "almost entirely" run by robots, guests "report problems", and "managers have replaced some of the robots with humans".`,passage:`A new type of hotel in Japan is run almost entirely by robots. A robot at reception greets guests, another carries their luggage and a third cleans the rooms. The hotel was built to save money and to attract visitors who are curious about technology. Guests say the experience is amusing, but they also report problems: the receptionist robot sometimes cannot understand names, and the luggage robot is slow on carpets. Managers have replaced some of the robots with humans, and the hotel is now a mixture of machines and people.`,statements:[{text:`The hotel is run only by robots.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some guests had problems with the robots.`,answer:!0},{text:`Managers have added human staff to replace some robots.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-199`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "around half... could disappear", linguists are "working with communities to record words", and schools "have helped Maori to recover".`,passage:`Language experts estimate that around half of the world's seven thousand languages could disappear by the end of the century. When the last speakers die, with them goes a unique way of describing the world. Linguists are working with communities to record words, songs and stories. Some communities have gone further and created schools where children are taught in the endangered language. In New Zealand, such schools have helped Maori to recover, and more people now speak it than a generation ago.`,statements:[{text:`Half of the world's languages may disappear.`,answer:!0},{text:`Linguists are ignoring endangered languages.`,answer:!1},{text:`Schools have helped Maori to recover.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-200`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says he "had always relied on takeaway", he practised "one dish a week", and he "invites friends for dinner every Saturday".`,passage:`Learning to cook was not easy for Daniel, who had always relied on takeaway food. His first attempts at pasta were sticky, and his soup was so salty that no one could finish it. Instead of giving up, he started watching online videos and practising one dish a week. After six months, he could prepare ten meals confidently. He also discovered that cooking calmed him after a busy day. He now invites friends for dinner every Saturday and says that nothing makes him prouder than hearing someone ask for a second helping.`,statements:[{text:`Daniel was an experienced cook before the story began.`,answer:!1},{text:`Daniel practised one dish a week.`,answer:!0},{text:`Daniel cooks only for himself.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-201`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "beneath a car park", the find "has delayed plans to build offices", and "local residents are delighted".`,passage:`Archaeologists have discovered the remains of an ancient settlement beneath a car park in the centre of a modern city. The site contains the foundations of houses, pieces of pottery and a well that is more than two thousand years old. The find has delayed plans to build offices on the land. The city council has decided to preserve the remains and to build a small museum above them. Some developers have complained about the delay and the cost, but local residents are delighted.`,statements:[{text:`The remains were found beneath a car park.`,answer:!0},{text:`The offices were built as planned.`,answer:!1},{text:`Local residents are unhappy about the discovery.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-202`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "people in their forties and fifties", luggage is "transported by van", and "the main risk is the weather".`,passage:`Cycling holidays are growing in popularity, particularly among people in their forties and fifties who want to stay active. A typical tour covers about fifty kilometres a day, with the luggage transported by van from one hotel to the next. Operators offer routes of different difficulty, so even beginners can join. The main risk is the weather: a day of wind and rain can turn a pleasant ride into a struggle. Experienced cyclists recommend waterproof clothes and a flexible attitude, since the schedule may change at short notice.`,statements:[{text:`Cycling holidays are mostly for teenagers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Luggage is carried by vehicle between hotels.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experienced cyclists say the weather is never a problem.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-203`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people "underestimate how long it takes", movers advise "labelling every box", and moving unwanted things "is a waste".`,passage:`Moving house is considered one of the most stressful events in life, along with changing jobs and losing a loved one. People often underestimate how long it takes to pack, and many reach the last night with half the kitchen still in cupboards. Professional movers advise starting weeks in advance, labelling every box with its contents and the room it belongs to, and keeping an overnight bag with essentials. They also suggest sorting out belongings before packing, since moving unwanted things is a waste of time and money.`,statements:[{text:`People usually overestimate the time needed to pack.`,answer:!1},{text:`Movers recommend labelling boxes.`,answer:!0},{text:`Movers say it is better to pack everything, including unwanted things.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-204`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the boat does not move", "the walls are thin, so noise can be a problem", and "planning to open a second one".`,passage:`A few years ago, a hotel in Amsterdam began to offer rooms in a converted boat. Guests sleep in cabins on the canal, wake up to the sound of water and eat breakfast on deck. The boat does not move, but its position near the city centre makes it very convenient. The rooms are small, and the walls are thin, so noise can be a problem. Even so, the boat is usually fully booked, and the owners are planning to open a second one.`,statements:[{text:`The hotel boat travels along the canals.`,answer:!1},{text:`Noise can be a problem in the boat hotel.`,answer:!0},{text:`The owners plan to open another boat.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-205`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the food has "damaged packaging", "two benefits: it reduces waste and feeds those in need", and "finding enough drivers" is the greatest difficulty.`,passage:`A charity in Manchester collects food that supermarkets cannot sell because the packaging is damaged or the sell-by date is close. Volunteers sort it and deliver it to community kitchens where people on low incomes can eat for free or for a small donation. Last year they saved more than five hundred tonnes of food from being thrown away. The organisers say that the project has two benefits: it reduces waste and feeds those in need. Their greatest difficulty is finding enough drivers, since most volunteers are retired and the work is physically demanding.`,statements:[{text:`The charity only collects food with perfect packaging.`,answer:!1},{text:`The project helps both to reduce waste and to feed people.`,answer:!0},{text:`The charity has plenty of drivers.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-206`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "fewer than ten per cent of students finish", group courses "have much better rates", and the reason is "the lack of a teacher".`,passage:`Online learning platforms offer courses in everything from coding to cooking, and millions of people have enrolled. The courses are often cheap or free, which is a great advantage. However, completion rates are low: fewer than ten per cent of students finish. Researchers think that the lack of a teacher to give encouragement and set deadlines is the main reason. Courses that include group projects or regular live sessions have much better rates, since students feel responsible to others.`,statements:[{text:`Most online students finish their courses.`,answer:!1},{text:`Group projects improve completion rates.`,answer:!0},{text:`The main reason for low completion is the high price.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-207`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says students "received written comments", the dropout number "fell", and the experiment will continue "before deciding whether to extend it".`,passage:`The first university in the country to introduce a "no-grade" first year reported unexpected results. Instead of marks, students received written comments on their progress. Many lecturers were worried that students would put in less effort, but attendance actually rose, and the number of students who dropped out fell. Those who disliked the system said they felt uncertain about where they stood. The university has decided to continue the experiment for another two years before deciding whether to extend it to other courses.`,statements:[{text:`Students received marks as usual in the first year.`,answer:!1},{text:`The number of students leaving the course decreased.`,answer:!0},{text:`The university has already extended the system to all courses.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-208`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says analysts "study large amounts of information", explaining results "is just as important", and courses "include presentation skills".`,passage:`A new type of job has appeared in many companies: the data analyst. These specialists study large amounts of information to help managers understand customers and predict trends. The work requires knowledge of statistics and programming, but employers say that the ability to explain results clearly is just as important. A brilliant analyst who cannot communicate findings in plain language is of little use to a team. Training courses now include presentation skills as well as technical subjects.`,statements:[{text:`Data analysts study large amounts of information.`,answer:!0},{text:`Technical knowledge is the only skill needed.`,answer:!1},{text:`Courses include presentation skills.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-209`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says shops were "dusty places", young shoppers "make up a growing share", and managers worry people "sell their unwanted clothes online".`,passage:`Charity shops have always sold second-hand goods, but their image has changed. Once seen as dusty places for people on low incomes, many are now stylish, with window displays and well-organised racks. Young shoppers, interested in vintage fashion and the environment, make up a growing share of customers. Volunteers say that the shops also play a social role, offering elderly people a place to talk. Some managers worry that quality donations are declining because people now sell their unwanted clothes online instead.`,statements:[{text:`Charity shops used to have a better image than now.`,answer:!1},{text:`Younger customers are increasingly visiting the shops.`,answer:!0},{text:`Online sales may reduce the quality of donations.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-210`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the channels carry water "from the wet north to the dry south", some routes "run along steep drops", and guides recommend "starting early".`,passage:`The island of Madeira is known for its levadas, narrow channels that carry water from the wet north to the dry south. Walking along them is popular with tourists, because the paths are mostly flat and pass through spectacular valleys. Some routes, however, run along steep drops, and visitors without a good head for heights should avoid them. Guides recommend starting early in the morning, before the mist rises, and carrying a torch in case a path leads through a tunnel. Hikers should also bring warm clothes, since the weather can change within an hour.`,statements:[{text:`The levadas bring water from the south to the north.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some routes are unsuitable for people afraid of heights.`,answer:!0},{text:`Guides suggest walking late in the afternoon.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-211`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says many procedures allow patients to "go home within hours", "moving early reduces the risk of complications", and nurses "telephone every patient the following day".`,passage:`In the past, hospitals were often places where patients lay in bed for weeks after an operation. Today, many procedures are carried out as day surgery, and patients go home within hours. Doctors say that moving early reduces the risk of complications, and patients usually recover better in their own beds. Not all operations are suitable: people with heart problems or those who live far from a hospital may need to stay overnight. Nurses telephone every patient the following day to check their progress.`,statements:[{text:`Patients today always stay in hospital for weeks.`,answer:!1},{text:`Early movement reduces the risk of complications.`,answer:!0},{text:`Nurses ignore patients after they leave.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-212`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she had "twenty years of experience", the writer "felt calmer" after ten minutes, and has "booked another lesson".`,passage:`The first time I rode a horse, I was nervous and the animal seemed enormous. The instructor, a patient woman with twenty years of experience, told me to relax my shoulders and breathe slowly. She explained that horses can sense fear. After ten minutes I felt calmer, and the horse began to respond to my instructions. By the end of the hour, we were trotting round the field, and I was smiling. I have booked another lesson for next week.`,statements:[{text:`The instructor was an inexperienced woman.`,answer:!1},{text:`The writer felt calmer after ten minutes.`,answer:!0},{text:`The writer has decided not to ride again.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-213`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the average age "has risen sharply", economists "warn that such policies may push prices higher", and some "choose to rent for life".`,passage:`In many countries, the average age of first-time home buyers has risen sharply. Young people face high prices, low wages and strict rules from banks, which usually ask for a large deposit. As a result, many remain in their parents' houses well into their thirties. Some governments have tried to help by offering loans with lower interest rates, but economists warn that such policies may simply push prices even higher. A growing number of young adults are therefore choosing to rent for life and to spend their savings on travel and education instead.`,statements:[{text:`Young people today generally buy their first home earlier than before.`,answer:!1},{text:`Economists are certain that cheap loans will solve the problem.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some young adults prefer to rent permanently.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-214`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says libraries opened "in the mid-nineteenth century", "hundreds of branches have closed", and campaigners argue they are "more important than ever".`,passage:`The first public libraries in Britain opened in the mid-nineteenth century, when reformers argued that working people should have access to books. Many people at the time feared that free reading would encourage unrest. In practice, the libraries became popular meeting places, offering newspapers and quiet space as well as books. Today, councils face financial pressure, and hundreds of branches have closed. Campaigners argue that libraries are more important than ever in an age of misinformation, because librarians help people to find reliable sources.`,statements:[{text:`Public libraries opened in Britain in the nineteenth century.`,answer:!0},{text:`Hundreds of library branches have recently closed.`,answer:!0},{text:`Campaigners think libraries are less important now.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-215`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says that city bees "often produce more honey", that "too many hives can leave the insects competing for food", and that councils "require beekeepers to register".`,passage:`Urban beekeeping has become surprisingly popular in several European capitals. Hives are placed on the roofs of offices, hotels and even theatres, where bees can feed on the flowers of parks and private gardens. Surprisingly, city bees often produce more honey than their rural cousins, because the countryside has fewer flowers after intensive farming removed hedges and wild meadows. However, experts warn that too many hives can leave the insects competing for food, which harms wild pollinators. Some councils now require beekeepers to register their hives and to plant flowers nearby so that the supply of nectar keeps pace with demand.`,statements:[{text:`Rural bees always produce more honey than city bees.`,answer:!1},{text:`Too many hives can create competition for food.`,answer:!0},{text:`Beekeepers in some cities must register their hives.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-216`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the shells "were used as beads", "some scientists... are cautious", and "further excavation is planned".`,passage:`Archaeologists in a cave in southern Africa have found shells that were used as beads about seventy-five thousand years ago. The beads have small holes and traces of red colouring, suggesting that they were worn as decorations. The discovery shows that early humans were capable of symbolic thought much earlier than previously believed. Some scientists, however, are cautious, pointing out that the shells might have been used for another purpose. Further excavation is planned to search for more evidence.`,statements:[{text:`The shells were probably used as beads.`,answer:!0},{text:`All scientists are completely certain about the use of the shells.`,answer:!1},{text:`Further excavation is planned.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-217`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says he was "exhausted" but the shop was popular, "sales rose by a third", and it was "the best decision of his career".`,passage:`When Karim started his own bakery, he worked eighteen hours a day. He baked the bread at night, served customers in the morning and did the accounts in the afternoon. After a year, the shop was popular but he was exhausted and had no time for his family. A friend advised him to hire two assistants, even though he thought he could not afford them. To his surprise, the extra staff allowed him to open for longer hours and sales rose by a third. Karim now says that the best decision of his career was to stop doing everything himself.`,statements:[{text:`Karim hired assistants because his business was failing.`,answer:!1},{text:`The shop earned more after he hired help.`,answer:!0},{text:`Karim regrets his decision to employ staff.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-218`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it "is different", freezing means "a home-cooked meal is available in minutes", and writers recommend "small amounts of fresh ingredients".`,passage:`Learning to cook for one person is different from cooking for a family. Single cooks often feel that it is not worth making an effort, and they end up eating ready meals. Food writers suggest cooking a larger quantity and freezing portions, so that on busy evenings a home-cooked meal is available in minutes. They also recommend buying small amounts of fresh ingredients and planning meals that share them, to avoid waste. Those who follow this advice often say they save money and eat more healthily.`,statements:[{text:`Cooking for one is the same as cooking for a family.`,answer:!1},{text:`Freezing portions can save time.`,answer:!0},{text:`Food writers advise buying large amounts of fresh food.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-219`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says cooperatives are "owned by their members", critics say "decision-making can be slow", and "hundreds of millions of people... belong to at least one".`,passage:`Cooperatives, businesses owned by their members, are common in agriculture, banking and housing. Members share profits and have a say in decisions, usually on the principle of one member, one vote. Supporters say that cooperatives are more stable in crises because they are not under pressure from outside shareholders. Critics argue that decision-making can be slow and that it is hard to raise capital. Despite these difficulties, hundreds of millions of people around the world belong to at least one cooperative.`,statements:[{text:`Cooperatives are owned by their members.`,answer:!0},{text:`Decisions in cooperatives are always made quickly.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many people belong to cooperatives.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-220`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "several coastal countries", "too many boats can disturb whales", and rules "set a minimum distance".`,passage:`Whale watching has become a major industry in several coastal countries. Boats take tourists out to sea and look for animals that come to feed or breed in the area. Operators claim that the trips raise awareness of marine conservation. However, researchers point out that too many boats can disturb whales, particularly mothers with calves. In response, some countries have introduced rules which limit the number of boats around an animal, set a minimum distance and ban trips during the breeding season.`,statements:[{text:`Whale watching trips take place only in one country.`,answer:!1},{text:`Too many boats can disturb whales.`,answer:!0},{text:`The rules allow boats to approach whales as closely as they wish.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-221`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "instead of focusing on one subject", participants are "surprised at how much more efficiently they study", and "some educators doubt".`,passage:`A new type of course teaches adults how to learn. Instead of focusing on one subject, the lessons explain how memory works and how to use techniques such as spacing, self-testing and summarising. Participants say they are surprised at how much more efficiently they study afterwards. The creators claim that the skills help in any career, since people today may need to learn new things many times. Some educators doubt whether general techniques can be separated from the subject being studied.`,statements:[{text:`The course focuses on a single subject.`,answer:!1},{text:`Participants say they study more efficiently afterwards.`,answer:!0},{text:`All educators agree the techniques work in every subject.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-222`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says forests "shrank", new forests "do not replace ancient ones", and trees "have grown back naturally" on abandoned farms.`,passage:`Throughout the twentieth century, the world's forests shrank as land was cleared for agriculture. In recent decades, however, the trend has begun to reverse in some regions. In parts of Europe and Asia, farms have been abandoned and trees have grown back naturally. Governments have also launched planting programmes. Environmental scientists welcome the change but warn that new forests do not replace ancient ones, which contain a far greater variety of plants and animals and store more carbon.`,statements:[{text:`Forests shrank in the twentieth century.`,answer:!0},{text:`New forests are as valuable as ancient ones.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some forests have grown back on abandoned farms.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-223`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "terrible at remembering names", the technique is "repeat the name immediately", and the writer remembered "eleven strangers".`,passage:`I have always been terrible at remembering names. At parties I introduce myself, listen to the answer and forget it within seconds. Last year, I read about a technique: repeat the name immediately, use it in your next sentence and link it with something familiar, such as a friend with the same name. It felt silly at first, but it worked. At a recent wedding I remembered the names of eleven strangers, and one of them was so impressed that she asked me to teach her the method.`,statements:[{text:`The writer has always been good at remembering names.`,answer:!1},{text:`The technique involves repeating the name.`,answer:!0},{text:`The writer remembered the names of eleven people at a wedding.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-224`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says residents "decided to save it", the cinema shows "classic films on weekends", and they asked "local businesses for support".`,passage:`The old cinema in our town was about to be demolished when a group of residents decided to save it. They raised money by organising film nights in the car park, selling tickets to neighbours and asking local businesses for support. After two years, they bought the building and began repairs. The cinema now shows classic films on weekends and hosts children's workshops during the week. The group's chairman says that the project has made the neighbourhood feel like a community again.`,statements:[{text:`The residents tried to save the cinema from being demolished.`,answer:!0},{text:`The cinema is now open every day for new releases.`,answer:!1},{text:`Local businesses were asked for support.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-225`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says adults need "a different approach", teachers advise "speak from the first lesson", and "fifteen minutes a day... is more effective".`,passage:`Learning a language as an adult requires a different approach from learning as a child. Adults have more discipline and can understand grammar rules, but they are often afraid of making mistakes. Children, on the other hand, imitate sounds without worrying about being wrong. Teachers advise adult learners to speak from the first lesson, even if the sentences are imperfect, and to listen to real conversations rather than only textbook dialogues. Most agree that regular contact with the language, even for fifteen minutes a day, is more effective than occasional long sessions.`,statements:[{text:`Adults learn languages in the same way as children.`,answer:!1},{text:`Teachers advise adults to speak from the beginning.`,answer:!0},{text:`Long occasional sessions are better than short daily contact.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-226`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "a piano was left for passengers to enjoy", the scheme "spread to airports and shopping centres", and passers-by "do not mind" the tuning.`,passage:`Many train stations now offer free pianos for anyone to play. The idea began in a small European station, where a piano was left for passengers to enjoy. People stopped to listen, and some started to sing along. Staff report that the piano has improved the mood in the station and that fewer people complain about delays. The instruments are sometimes out of tune, but passers-by say they do not mind. The scheme has spread to airports and shopping centres in dozens of countries.`,statements:[{text:`The piano in the station is meant for passengers to play.`,answer:!0},{text:`The scheme has stayed in only one station.`,answer:!1},{text:`Passengers complain that the pianos are slightly out of tune.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-227`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says traders began to sell "things that supermarkets could not offer", the complaint is "parking", and the council "is considering" a shuttle.`,passage:`The old market in our town has survived because it adapted. Fifty years ago it sold mainly fruit and vegetables, but when supermarkets opened, many stalls closed. The remaining traders began to sell things that supermarkets could not offer: handmade cheese, vintage clothes and freshly cooked street food. Today the market is busier at weekends than the shopping centre, and visitors come from nearby towns. The only complaint is parking, which is limited and expensive, so the council is considering a free shuttle bus from the railway station.`,statements:[{text:`The market now sells exactly the same goods as fifty years ago.`,answer:!1},{text:`Parking near the market is a problem.`,answer:!0},{text:`The shuttle bus service already exists.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-228`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says new employees "learn more quickly" in the office, productivity "has stayed stable", and some workers "feel more tired".`,passage:`Remote working, which became common during the pandemic, is now being reconsidered by many companies. Managers argue that new employees learn more quickly when they sit next to experienced colleagues, and that spontaneous conversations lead to good ideas. Employees reply that they save hours of commuting and can concentrate better at home. Several firms have adopted a hybrid model, in which staff come to the office on fixed days. Early results are mixed: productivity has stayed stable, but some workers say that they now feel more tired because they must be available both online and in person.`,statements:[{text:`Managers believe that office work helps new staff to learn.`,answer:!0},{text:`Productivity has fallen sharply under the hybrid model.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some employees feel more tired with the hybrid system.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-229`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says overnight visitors "are exempt", the money "will pay for cleaning and maintaining", and "residents are divided".`,passage:`The city of Venice has begun to charge day visitors an entrance fee during busy periods. The authorities say that the money will pay for cleaning and maintaining the fragile historic centre, and that the measure will discourage the large groups who spend only a few hours in the city and contribute little to the local economy. Residents are divided. Some think the fee is a sensible step, while others argue that a city should not be treated like a theme park. Visitors staying overnight in hotels are exempt.`,statements:[{text:`All visitors to Venice must pay the fee.`,answer:!1},{text:`The money is meant to help maintain the historic centre.`,answer:!0},{text:`All residents approve of the fee.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-230`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says airports "reduce the stress of travel", relaxed passengers "tend to spend more", and critics want money used "to improve security queues and baggage handling".`,passage:`Airports are making an effort to reduce the stress of travel. Some have installed quiet rooms where passengers can rest, play areas for children and even gardens with real plants. One airport in Asia has a swimming pool on the roof, and another offers free film screenings. These features also have a business purpose: airports earn money from shops and restaurants, and passengers who feel relaxed tend to spend more. Critics argue that the money would be better used to improve security queues and baggage handling, which are the main causes of complaint.`,statements:[{text:`Airports are trying to make travel less stressful.`,answer:!0},{text:`Relaxed passengers spend less.`,answer:!1},{text:`Critics say money should go to security queues and baggage handling.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-231`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says gardening reduces stress, beginners should choose "plants that are easy to look after", and too much water is "a common mistake".`,passage:`Gardening has been shown to reduce stress, and it also provides exercise and fresh food. Even people with no garden can take part by growing herbs on a windowsill or joining a community garden. Beginners are often advised to start with plants that are easy to look after, such as lettuce, radishes or mint. A common mistake is giving plants too much water. Most vegetables prefer soil that is damp but not wet, and experts recommend watering in the early morning.`,statements:[{text:`Gardening has no effect on stress levels.`,answer:!1},{text:`Beginners should choose plants that are easy to look after.`,answer:!0},{text:`Giving plants a lot of water is the best way to help them.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-232`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "there is a limit", students working "more than fifteen hours... tend to have lower marks", and universities advise jobs "on campus or in the holidays".`,passage:`Students who work part-time while at university often say that it teaches them valuable lessons. They learn to manage their time, deal with customers and take responsibility. However, research suggests there is a limit. Students who work more than fifteen hours a week tend to have lower marks and are more likely to drop out. Universities advise students to look for jobs on campus or in the holidays and to talk to their tutors if the workload becomes too heavy.`,statements:[{text:`Part-time work always improves student grades.`,answer:!1},{text:`Working more than fifteen hours a week can lower marks.`,answer:!0},{text:`Universities advise against all student jobs.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-233`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says libraries "also offer free internet access", that some residents have "no computer at home", and that "some readers complain".`,passage:`Public libraries are changing faster than most people realise. Besides lending books, many now offer free internet access, language classes and workshops on how to apply for jobs. In some districts, the library is the only place where residents without a computer at home can fill in official forms. Librarians say that their role has become more like that of social workers, helping people who feel lost in a world where services are increasingly online. Not everyone welcomes the shift. Some readers complain that the buildings are noisier than before, and they would prefer a quiet space dedicated entirely to books.`,statements:[{text:`Libraries only lend books nowadays.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some people use the library to complete official forms.`,answer:!0},{text:`All readers are happy with the changes in libraries.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-234`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says companies with "plants, natural light" saw satisfaction rise, workers with a view of trees recovered "more quickly", and changes "need not be expensive".`,passage:`The design of a workplace can influence how people feel. Companies that introduced plants, natural light and comfortable seating reported that employees were more satisfied and took fewer sick days. A study found that workers in rooms with a view of trees recovered from stress more quickly than those facing a wall. The changes need not be expensive. Even small steps, like allowing staff to personalise their desks, can create a greater sense of belonging, according to psychologists.`,statements:[{text:`Natural light and plants improved employee satisfaction.`,answer:!0},{text:`Workers facing a wall recovered from stress more quickly.`,answer:!1},{text:`Changes to workplaces must be expensive.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-235`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they can talk "without making an appointment", "only a few people use the opportunity each week", and "some staff remain nervous".`,passage:`The company's new open-door policy allows any employee to talk to the director without making an appointment. Managers feared that she would be overwhelmed, but in practice only a few people use the opportunity each week. Most come with practical suggestions, and several have led to changes in how work is organised. The director says that the biggest benefit is not the ideas themselves but the message that anyone's opinion can matter. Some staff remain nervous about speaking to someone so senior.`,statements:[{text:`Employees need an appointment to see the director.`,answer:!1},{text:`Only a few employees use the policy each week.`,answer:!0},{text:`All staff are comfortable speaking to the director.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-236`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it types "with very few errors", some users find it "difficult to speak in complete sentences", and it "struggles with... noisy environments".`,passage:`Voice recognition software has reached the point where it can type a spoken text with very few errors. Lawyers, doctors and journalists who once spent hours typing now dictate their reports. The software learns the speaker's voice and vocabulary over time. It still struggles with strong accents and noisy environments, however. A few users also report that it is difficult to speak in complete sentences without a script in front of them, so many revert to typing for creative writing.`,statements:[{text:`Voice recognition software makes many errors.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some users find dictating creative writing difficult.`,answer:!0},{text:`The software works equally well in noisy places.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-237`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "I would never sleep again... They were right", the baby was fascinated by "a leaf to a spoon", and "I now try to look at the world with the curiosity of a child".`,passage:`When I had my first child, everyone told me that I would never sleep again. They were right, but they did not tell me about the delight of watching her discover the world. Every small thing, from a leaf to a spoon, seemed to fascinate her. Looking at her helped me to notice things I had stopped seeing. My routine, once so familiar, felt fresh again. I now try to look at the world with the curiosity of a child, even on tired mornings.`,statements:[{text:`The writer got plenty of sleep after the baby arrived.`,answer:!1},{text:`The baby seemed fascinated by small things.`,answer:!0},{text:`The writer has learned to be more curious.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-238`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says artificial snow "uses large amounts of water and energy", resorts offer "hiking, mountain biking", and low-altitude resorts "are particularly vulnerable".`,passage:`The Alps are no longer reliably covered with snow in the early winter, and many ski resorts have been forced to adapt. Some have invested in machines that produce artificial snow, but this uses large amounts of water and energy. Others have decided to offer different activities, such as hiking, mountain biking and spa weekends, so that visitors are attracted all year round. Small low-altitude resorts are particularly vulnerable, and several have closed. Experts advise tourists to choose higher resorts if they want to be sure of snow.`,statements:[{text:`Artificial snow is cheap and environmentally friendly.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some resorts now attract visitors in summer with other activities.`,answer:!0},{text:`Low-altitude resorts are the safest for skiing.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-239`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it has "more bookshops per head than anywhere else", the festival attracts "tens of thousands", and shops survive "by offering rare editions and a pleasant place to browse".`,passage:`The little town of Hay-on-Wye, on the border between England and Wales, has more bookshops per head than anywhere else in the world. It began in the 1960s, when an enthusiastic bookseller bought up old libraries and opened several shops. Others followed, and the town became a destination for readers. Every year, a literary festival attracts tens of thousands of visitors, though some residents complain about traffic during the event. The bookshops have survived the rise of online sellers by offering rare editions and a pleasant place to browse.`,statements:[{text:`Hay-on-Wye is famous for its bookshops.`,answer:!0},{text:`The festival is attended by only a few visitors.`,answer:!1},{text:`The bookshops have survived because they offer rare editions and a place to browse.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-240`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says pedestrians "complain", the city "fixed their maximum speed", and the companies say the problem is "bad parking, not the vehicles".`,passage:`Electric scooters, available for rent in many cities, divide public opinion. Users like them because they are cheap and quick for short journeys. Pedestrians, however, complain that scooters are left on pavements, where they block wheelchairs and push chairs. After a number of accidents, one city banned scooters from the old centre and fixed their maximum speed at fifteen kilometres an hour elsewhere. The rental companies argue that the real problem is bad parking, not the vehicles, and they have started offering discounts to users who leave scooters in marked areas.`,statements:[{text:`Pedestrians generally welcome electric scooters.`,answer:!1},{text:`One city has limited the speed of the scooters.`,answer:!0},{text:`Rental companies blame the vehicles themselves for the problems.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-241`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the "job for life" has "largely disappeared", advisers recommend "keeping skills up to date", and some "find it unsettling".`,passage:`The idea of a "job for life" has largely disappeared. Workers who started their careers in the 1970s often stayed with the same company until retirement, but today people can expect to change employer many times. Some see this as an opportunity to develop new skills; others find it unsettling and worry about security. Careers advisers recommend keeping skills up to date, building a network of contacts and being willing to learn a new field when necessary. Employers, in turn, are discovering that experienced staff who change jobs bring fresh perspectives.`,statements:[{text:`Most workers today stay with one company for life.`,answer:!1},{text:`Advisers recommend keeping skills up to date.`,answer:!0},{text:`All workers feel positive about frequent job changes.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-242`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it closed "after only eighteen months", the new place "serves ordinary dishes with meat", and her aim is "to attract meat-eaters who want to try something new".`,passage:`The first fully vegetarian restaurant in the town closed last year after only eighteen months. The owner blamed high rents and lack of customers, but others suggested that the menu had been too limited. A new vegetarian place has since opened on the same street with a very different approach: it serves ordinary dishes with meat, but offers more than a dozen plant-based options. The owner says that her aim is to attract meat-eaters who want to try something new, not to convert them.`,statements:[{text:`The first vegetarian restaurant lasted for several years.`,answer:!1},{text:`The new restaurant serves only vegetarian food.`,answer:!1},{text:`The new owner wants to attract meat-eaters.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-243`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says workers consider "half of them unnecessary", companies limit meetings "to thirty minutes", and people "finish quickly when they are on their feet".`,passage:`Meetings are often criticised as a waste of time. A recent survey found that office workers spend almost a third of their week in meetings and consider half of them unnecessary. Some companies have tried to change the culture: meetings are limited to thirty minutes, an agenda must be sent in advance, and people are encouraged to leave if they have nothing to contribute. One firm even introduced standing meetings, since people tend to finish quickly when they are on their feet.`,statements:[{text:`Workers think most meetings are necessary.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some companies limit the length of meetings.`,answer:!0},{text:`Standing meetings tend to be longer.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-244`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says handwriting "is disappearing", handwritten notes help students "understand lectures better", and typists "copy the lecturer's words without processing them".`,passage:`Handwriting is disappearing from classrooms as more schools give pupils tablets. Supporters of the change say that typing is faster and more useful for the workplace. Yet research by psychologists suggests that students who take notes by hand understand lectures better, because they cannot write everything and must decide what is important. Typists tend to copy the lecturer's words without processing them. Some universities now encourage students to take handwritten notes and to type them up later, combining the benefits of both methods.`,statements:[{text:`Handwriting is becoming more common in schools.`,answer:!1},{text:`Handwritten notes may lead to better understanding.`,answer:!0},{text:`Typists usually think carefully about each sentence.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-245`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says camping "can be stressful", campers "recommend packing a few board games", and children "will remember that mishap more fondly".`,passage:`Camping with a young family can be a stressful experience if you are not prepared. The tent must be put up before dark, children need entertainment when it rains, and a small stove is never as convenient as a kitchen. Experienced campers recommend packing a few board games, bringing more food than you think you need and choosing a campsite with toilets nearby. They also advise lowering your expectations: on the first night something will almost certainly go wrong, and children will remember that mishap more fondly than the perfectly planned moments.`,statements:[{text:`Camping with children is always relaxing.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experienced campers advise taking board games.`,answer:!0},{text:`The children will probably forget the problems.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-246`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "several projects led to new products", managers' fears were unfounded, and critics argue "it works only in creative industries".`,passage:`When a company introduced a policy of allowing employees to spend one afternoon a week on personal projects, managers feared a loss of productivity. In fact, several projects led to new products, and staff said they felt more motivated. The policy has since been copied by other firms. Critics argue that it works only in creative industries, where ideas are the main source of value. In jobs with fixed routines, such as cleaning or manufacturing, it may be impossible to give workers free time.`,statements:[{text:`The policy reduced productivity.`,answer:!1},{text:`Several personal projects led to new products.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics believe it works equally well in every industry.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-247`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says that unpaid work "favours students whose parents can support them", the law requires pay "if they do real work", and employers "warn" about fewer places.`,passage:`Internships are meant to give young people experience, but critics say that many companies use them as a source of free labour. In some industries it is almost impossible to get a paid job without having worked several unpaid months first, which favours students whose parents can support them. A new law in one country now requires employers to pay interns at least the minimum wage if they do real work. Employers' groups warn that fewer internships will be offered, while student unions say that this is a price worth paying for fairness.`,statements:[{text:`Internships are always paid in most industries.`,answer:!1},{text:`A new law requires payment for interns who do real work.`,answer:!0},{text:`Employers' groups support the new law enthusiastically.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-248`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the roofs have "sedum, a hardy plant that needs little water", and "other towns have contacted the council".`,passage:`A project in a Dutch city has turned the roofs of bus shelters into small gardens planted with sedum, a hardy plant that needs little water. The plants absorb dust from the air, attract bees and help to keep the temperature down in summer. Each shelter costs only a little more to build than a standard one, and maintenance is carried out twice a year by volunteers. The city plans to install two hundred more, and other towns have contacted the council to ask how it was done.`,statements:[{text:`The roofs are planted with sedum.`,answer:!0},{text:`The shelters need daily watering.`,answer:!1},{text:`Other towns have shown interest in the idea.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-249`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says noise "not too loud and not too quiet" helps, the effect "disappears" above seventy decibels, and "several websites now offer recordings".`,passage:`A great many people have a favourite coffee shop where they do their best thinking. The background noise, researchers say, is not too loud and not too quiet, and it helps the brain to concentrate on creative tasks. The effect disappears when the noise becomes louder than about seventy decibels. Several websites now offer recordings of coffee-shop sounds for those who work at home, and many users claim that they are surprisingly effective, although others find that real conversations are more stimulating.`,statements:[{text:`Moderate background noise may help creative thinking.`,answer:!0},{text:`Very loud noise improves concentration even more.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some websites provide coffee-shop sounds.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-250`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says highlighting students "remembered no more", the best methods included "testing yourself", and teachers advise students to "close the book".`,passage:`Many students believe that highlighting the main points of a text with a coloured pen is an effective way to study. Research, however, suggests otherwise. In experiments, students who simply highlighted passages remembered no more than students who had only read them. The most successful methods were testing yourself with questions and spreading study sessions over several days. Teachers now advise students to close the book after reading a chapter and to try to write down everything they can remember, because that effort strengthens memory.`,statements:[{text:`Highlighting is the most effective method of studying.`,answer:!1},{text:`Self-testing helps people remember.`,answer:!0},{text:`Teachers advise students to read a chapter many times without stopping.`,answer:!1}]}]};export{e as default};